(function(m,c){var L=window.ACADEMY.lessons;for(var id in c){Object.assign(L[id],c[id]);}(window.ACADEMY_LOADED=window.ACADEMY_LOADED||{})[m]=1;})("sql-ops",{"sql-ops/reading-explain-and-finding-slow-queries":{"html":"\u003cp>Most database slowness comes from a small number of queries. The work is to find them, read what the database does with them, and change one thing at a time. The examples use PostgreSQL, and the ideas carry over to MySQL and others.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"what-you-will-learn\">What you will learn\u003c/h2>\n\u003cul>\n\u003cli>Finding expensive queries with pg_stat_statements and the slow query log\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Reading EXPLAIN and EXPLAIN ANALYZE\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Sequential scan, index scan and join types\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>What stale statistics look like\u003c/li>\n\u003c/ul>\n\u003ch2 id=\"find-the-queries-first\">Find the queries first\u003c/h2>\n\u003cp>Enable the \u003ccode>pg_stat_statements\u003c/code> extension. It records every normalized query with how many times it ran and how long it took.\u003c/p>\n\u003cdiv class=\"code console\" data-lang=\"terminal\">\u003cbutton class=\"copy\" type=\"button\" data-copy=\"psql -c &quot;SELECT calls, round(total_exec_time::numeric,0) AS total_ms, round(mean_exec_time::numeric,1) AS mean_ms, left(query,60) AS query FROM pg_stat_statements ORDER BY total_exec_time DESC LIMIT 3;&quot;\" aria-label=\"Copy commands\">Copy commands\u003c/button>\u003cpre tabindex=\"0\">\u003ccode class=\"hljs\">\u003cspan class=\"ln cmd\">\u003cspan class=\"ps\" aria-hidden=\"true\">$ \u003c/span>psql -c \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">&quot;SELECT calls, round(total_exec_time::numeric,0) AS total_ms, round(mean_exec_time::numeric,1) AS mean_ms, left(query,60) AS query FROM pg_stat_statements ORDER BY total_exec_time DESC LIMIT 3;&quot;\u003c/span>\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln out\"> calls  | total_ms | mean_ms | query\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln out\">--------+----------+---------+--------------------------------------------------\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln out\"> 480211 |  9120432 |    19.0 | SELECT * FROM orders WHERE customer_id = $1 ORDER BY\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln out\">   1210 |  4411200 |  3645.5 | SELECT customer_id, sum(total) FROM orders WHERE cre\u003c/span>\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>\n\u003cp>Sort by \u003cstrong>total\u003c/strong> time, not by the slowest single run. A 19 ms query that runs 480 000 times costs more than a 3.6 s report that runs 1 200 times. The setting \u003ccode>log_min_duration_statement = 500\u003c/code> writes any statement slower than 500 ms to the log, which catches queries your extension view has not reached yet.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"explain\">EXPLAIN\u003c/h2>\n\u003cp>\u003ccode>EXPLAIN\u003c/code> shows the plan the database chooses. \u003ccode>EXPLAIN (ANALYZE, BUFFERS)\u003c/code> runs the query and shows what really happened. Do not use ANALYZE on a statement that changes data without a transaction you roll back.\u003c/p>\n\u003cdiv class=\"code console\" data-lang=\"terminal\">\u003cbutton class=\"copy\" type=\"button\" data-copy=\"psql -c &quot;EXPLAIN (ANALYZE, BUFFERS) SELECT * FROM orders WHERE customer_id = 4711;&quot;\" aria-label=\"Copy commands\">Copy commands\u003c/button>\u003cpre tabindex=\"0\">\u003ccode class=\"hljs\">\u003cspan class=\"ln cmd\">\u003cspan class=\"ps\" aria-hidden=\"true\">$ \u003c/span>psql -c \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">&quot;EXPLAIN (ANALYZE, BUFFERS) SELECT * FROM orders WHERE customer_id = 4711;&quot;\u003c/span>\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln out\"> Seq Scan on orders  (cost=0.00..412345.00 rows=11 width=96) (actual time=0.031..1893.2 rows=9 loops=1)\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln out\">   Filter: (customer_id = 4711)\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln out\">   Rows Removed by Filter: 9999991\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln out\">   Buffers: shared hit=1204 read=211141\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln out\"> Planning Time: 0.12 ms\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln out\"> Execution Time: 1893.3 ms\u003c/span>\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>\n\u003cp>Read it from the inside out and look for:\u003c/p>\n\u003cul>\n\u003cli>\u003cstrong>Seq Scan\u003c/strong> on a big table that returns a few rows. The database read ten million rows to find nine (\u003ccode>Rows Removed by Filter\u003c/code>). An index on \u003ccode>customer_id\u003c/code> would let it jump to them.\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>\u003cstrong>Index Scan / Index Only Scan / Bitmap Heap Scan\u003c/strong>: the database uses an index. Fewer buffers read is the goal.\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>\u003cstrong>Estimated rows vs actual rows\u003c/strong>: \u003ccode>rows=11\u003c/code> versus \u003ccode>rows=9\u003c/code> is fine. When the estimate is 10 and the actual is 2 000 000, the planner made its choice with wrong information. Run \u003ccode>ANALYZE table;\u003c/code> to refresh statistics.\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>\u003cstrong>Sort\u003c/strong> with \u003ccode>Sort Method: external merge Disk\u003c/code> means the sort spilled to disk because \u003ccode>work_mem\u003c/code> was too small for that query.\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>\u003cstrong>Nested Loop\u003c/strong> is good for few rows and terrible for many. \u003cstrong>Hash Join\u003c/strong> and \u003cstrong>Merge Join\u003c/strong> suit larger sets.\u003c/li>\n\u003c/ul>\n\u003ch2 id=\"change-one-thing\">Change one thing\u003c/h2>\n\u003cp>After a change, run the same EXPLAIN again and compare the time and the buffers. Keep the old plan text in your ticket, so a reviewer can see the difference.\u003c/p>\n\u003cdiv class=\"code console\" data-lang=\"terminal\">\u003cbutton class=\"copy\" type=\"button\" data-copy=\"psql -c &quot;CREATE INDEX CONCURRENTLY idx_orders_customer ON orders (customer_id);&quot;\npsql -c &quot;EXPLAIN (ANALYZE) SELECT * FROM orders WHERE customer_id = 4711;&quot; | grep -E &quot;Index|Execution&quot;\" aria-label=\"Copy commands\">Copy commands\u003c/button>\u003cpre tabindex=\"0\">\u003ccode class=\"hljs\">\u003cspan class=\"ln cmd\">\u003cspan class=\"ps\" aria-hidden=\"true\">$ \u003c/span>psql -c \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">&quot;CREATE INDEX CONCURRENTLY idx_orders_customer ON orders (customer_id);&quot;\u003c/span>\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln out\">CREATE INDEX\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln cmd\">\u003cspan class=\"ps\" aria-hidden=\"true\">$ \u003c/span>psql -c \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">&quot;EXPLAIN (ANALYZE) SELECT * FROM orders WHERE customer_id = 4711;&quot;\u003c/span> | grep -E \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">&quot;Index|Execution&quot;\u003c/span>\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln out\"> Index Scan using idx_orders_customer on orders  (actual time=0.040..0.061 rows=9 loops=1)\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln out\"> Execution Time: 0.09 ms\u003c/span>\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>\n\u003cp>\u003ccode>CONCURRENTLY\u003c/code> builds the index without blocking writes. It takes longer and cannot run inside a transaction block.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"how-the-parts-fit-together\">How the parts fit together\u003c/h2>\n\u003cp>The lesson in one picture: each box is a section of this lesson, in the order you meet it. Select a box to see what it does and the mistake that usually goes with it.\u003c/p>\n\u003cfigure class=\"dg\" data-rows=\"2\" data-edges=\"n1&gt;n2;n2&gt;n3\" style=\"--rows:2\">\u003cfigcaption class=\"dg-title\">How Reading EXPLAIN and finding slow queries fits together\u003c/figcaption>\u003cdiv class=\"dg-stage\">\u003csvg viewBox=\"0 0 100 100\" preserveAspectRatio=\"none\" aria-hidden=\"true\" focusable=\"false\">\u003cline x1=\"16.7\" y1=\"25.0\" x2=\"50.0\" y2=\"75.0\"/>\u003cline x1=\"50.0\" y1=\"75.0\" x2=\"83.3\" y2=\"25.0\"/>\u003c/svg>\u003cbutton type=\"button\" class=\"dg-node k-process\" data-act=\"dg-pick\" data-id=\"n1\" data-kind=\"process\" style=\"left:16.7%;top:25.0%;--w:29.3%\" aria-pressed=\"false\">\u003cspan class=\"dg-name\">Find the queries first\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"dg-kind\">Step\u003c/span>\u003c/button>\u003cbutton type=\"button\" class=\"dg-node k-process\" data-act=\"dg-pick\" data-id=\"n2\" data-kind=\"process\" style=\"left:50.0%;top:75.0%;--w:29.3%\" aria-pressed=\"false\">\u003cspan class=\"dg-name\">EXPLAIN\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"dg-kind\">Step\u003c/span>\u003c/button>\u003cbutton type=\"button\" class=\"dg-node k-process\" data-act=\"dg-pick\" data-id=\"n3\" data-kind=\"process\" style=\"left:83.3%;top:25.0%;--w:29.3%\" aria-pressed=\"false\">\u003cspan class=\"dg-name\">Change one thing\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"dg-kind\">Step\u003c/span>\u003c/button>\u003c/div>\u003cp class=\"sr\">Flow in order: 1, Find the queries first to EXPLAIN; 2, EXPLAIN to Change one thing.\u003c/p>\u003cul class=\"dg-legend\" aria-label=\"Legend\">\u003cli>\u003ci class=\"dg-sw k-process\" aria-hidden=\"true\">\u003c/i>Step\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003ci class=\"dg-arrow\" aria-hidden=\"true\">\u003c/i>Numbered arrows show the order of the flow\u003c/li>\u003c/ul>\u003cp class=\"dg-hint small\">Select a component to see what it does and what happens when it fails. \u003cbutton type=\"button\" class=\"btn btn-quiet dg-flow-btn\" data-act=\"dg-flow\" aria-pressed=\"false\">Show the flow\u003c/button>\u003c/p>\u003cdiv class=\"dg-live\" role=\"status\" aria-live=\"polite\">\u003c/div>\u003cdl class=\"dg-list\">\u003cdiv class=\"dg-item\" data-id=\"n1\">\u003cdt>Find the queries first \u003cspan class=\"dg-kt\">Step\u003c/span>\u003c/dt>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">What it does:\u003c/span> Enable the pg_stat_statements extension.\u003c/dd>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">If it fails or is weak:\u003c/span> Tuning the slowest single query and ignoring the one that runs a million times.\u003c/dd>\u003c/div>\u003cdiv class=\"dg-item\" data-id=\"n2\">\u003cdt>EXPLAIN \u003cspan class=\"dg-kt\">Step\u003c/span>\u003c/dt>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">What it does:\u003c/span> EXPLAIN shows the plan the database chooses.\u003c/dd>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">If it fails or is weak:\u003c/span> Running EXPLAIN ANALYZE on writes in production without a rollback.\u003c/dd>\u003c/div>\u003cdiv class=\"dg-item\" data-id=\"n3\">\u003cdt>Change one thing \u003cspan class=\"dg-kt\">Step\u003c/span>\u003c/dt>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">What it does:\u003c/span> After a change, run the same EXPLAIN again and compare the time and the buffers.\u003c/dd>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">If it fails or is weak:\u003c/span> Adding indexes before reading the plan.\u003c/dd>\u003c/div>\u003c/dl>\u003c/figure>\n\u003ch2 id=\"incident-the-query-that-was-fast-yesterday\">Incident: The query that was fast yesterday\u003c/h2>\n\u003caside class=\"callout callout-incident\">\u003cdiv class=\"callout-title\">Incident\u003c/div>\u003cp>A checkout query went from 5 ms to 4 seconds overnight with no code change.\u003c/p>\n\u003c/aside>\n\u003cdiv class=\"code console\" data-lang=\"terminal\">\u003cbutton class=\"copy\" type=\"button\" data-copy=\"psql -c &quot;EXPLAIN (ANALYZE) SELECT * FROM carts WHERE status='open' AND user_id=88;&quot;\npsql -c &quot;SELECT relname, last_autoanalyze, n_live_tup FROM pg_stat_user_tables WHERE relname='carts';&quot;\" aria-label=\"Copy commands\">Copy commands\u003c/button>\u003cpre tabindex=\"0\">\u003ccode class=\"hljs\">\u003cspan class=\"ln cmd\">\u003cspan class=\"ps\" aria-hidden=\"true\">$ \u003c/span>psql -c \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">&quot;EXPLAIN (ANALYZE) SELECT * FROM carts WHERE status=&#x27;open&#x27; AND user_id=88;&quot;\u003c/span>\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln out\"> Nested Loop  (cost=0.85..120.40 rows=3 width=64) (actual time=0.03..4012.7 rows=118002 loops=1)\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln cmd\">\u003cspan class=\"ps\" aria-hidden=\"true\">$ \u003c/span>psql -c \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">&quot;SELECT relname, last_autoanalyze, n_live_tup FROM pg_stat_user_tables WHERE relname=&#x27;carts&#x27;;&quot;\u003c/span>\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln out\"> carts | 2026-10-19 02:11:04 | 3120\u003c/span>\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>\n\u003cp>The planner expected 3 rows and found 118 002. The table had been loaded with a large import the night before, and statistics were two weeks old, so the planner chose a nested loop that suited a small table. \u003ccode>ANALYZE carts;\u003c/code> fixed the plan in seconds. The lesson is to compare estimated and actual rows before adding indexes: a missing index and stale statistics look similar and have different cures.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"common-mistakes\">Common mistakes\u003c/h2>\n\u003cul>\n\u003cli>Tuning the slowest single query and ignoring the one that runs a million times\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Adding indexes before reading the plan\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Running EXPLAIN ANALYZE on writes in production without a rollback\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Judging by a query on a small development database\u003c/li>\n\u003c/ul>\n","toc":[{"id":"what-you-will-learn","text":"What you will learn"},{"id":"find-the-queries-first","text":"Find the queries first"},{"id":"explain","text":"EXPLAIN"},{"id":"change-one-thing","text":"Change one thing"},{"id":"how-the-parts-fit-together","text":"How the parts fit together"},{"id":"incident-the-query-that-was-fast-yesterday","text":"Incident: The query that was fast yesterday"},{"id":"common-mistakes","text":"Common mistakes"}],"refs":[{"t":"PostgreSQL: Using EXPLAIN","u":"https://www.postgresql.org/docs/current/using-explain.html"},{"t":"PostgreSQL: pg_stat_statements","u":"https://www.postgresql.org/docs/current/pgstatstatements.html"},{"t":"Use The Index, Luke","u":"https://use-the-index-luke.com/"}],"text":"Most database slowness comes from a small number of queries. The work is to find them, read what the database does with them, and change one thing at a time. The examples use PostgreSQL, and the ideas carry over to MySQL and others. What you will learn Finding expensive queries with pg_stat_statements and the slow query log Reading EXPLAIN and EXPLAIN ANALYZE Sequential scan, index scan and join types What stale statistics look like Find the queries first Enable the pg_stat_statements extension. It records every normalized query with how many times it ran and how long it took. $ psql -c \"SELECT calls, round(total_exec_time::numeric,0) AS total_ms, round(mean_exec_time::numeric,1) AS mean_ms, left(query,60) AS query FROM pg_stat_statements ORDER BY total_exec_time DESC LIMIT 3;\" calls | total_ms | mean_ms | query --------+----------+---------+-------------------------------------------------- 480211 | 9120432 | 19.0 | SELECT * FROM orders WHERE customer_id = $1 ORDER BY 1210 | 4411200 | 3645.5 | SELECT customer_id, sum(total) FROM orders WHERE cre Sort by total time, not by the slowest single run. A 19 ms query that runs 480 000 times costs more than a 3.6 s report that runs 1 200 times. The setting log_min_duration_statement = 500 writes any statement slower than 500 ms to the log, which catches queries your extension view has not reached yet. EXPLAIN EXPLAIN shows the plan the database chooses. EXPLAIN (ANALYZE, BUFFERS) runs the query and shows what really happened. Do not use ANALYZE on a statement that changes data without a transaction you roll back. $ psql -c \"EXPLAIN (ANALYZE, BUFFERS) SELECT * FROM orders WHERE customer_id = 4711;\" Seq Scan on orders (cost=0.00..412345.00 rows=11 width=96) (actual time=0.031..1893.2 rows=9 loops=1) Filter: (customer_id = 4711) Rows Removed by Filter: 9999991 Buffers: shared hit=1204 read=211141 Planning Time: 0.12 ms Execution Time: 1893.3 ms Read it from the inside out and look for: Seq Scan on a big table that returns a few rows. The database read ten million rows to find nine ( Rows Removed by Filter ). An index on customer_id would let it jump to them. Index Scan / Index Only Scan / Bitmap Heap Scan : the database uses an index. Fewer buffers read is the goal. Estimated rows vs actual rows : rows=11 versus rows=9 is fine. When the estimate is 10 and the actual is 2 000 000, the planner made its choice with wrong information. Run ANALYZE table; to refresh statistics. Sort with Sort Method: external merge Disk means the sort spilled to disk because work_mem was too small for that query. Nested Loop is good for few rows and terrible for many. Hash Join and Merge Join suit larger sets. Change one thing After a change, run the same EXPLAIN again and compare the time and the buffers. Keep the old plan text in your ticket, so a reviewer can see the difference. $ psql -c \"CREATE INDEX CONCURRENTLY idx_orders_customer ON orders (customer_id);\" CREATE INDEX $ psql -c \"EXPLAIN (ANALYZE) SELECT * FROM orders WHERE customer_id = 4711;\" | grep -E \"Index|Execution\" Index Scan using idx_orders_customer on orders (actual time=0.040..0.061 rows=9 loops=1) Execution Time: 0.09 ms CONCURRENTLY builds the index without blocking writes. It takes longer and cannot run inside a transaction block. How the parts fit together The lesson in one picture: each box is a section of this lesson, in the order you meet it. Select a box to see what it does and the mistake that usually goes with it. How Reading EXPLAIN and finding slow queries fits together Flow in order: 1, Find the queries first to EXPLAIN; 2, EXPLAIN to Change one thing. Step Numbered arrows show the order of the flow Select a component to see what it does and what happens when it fails. Find the queries first Step What it does: Enable the pg_stat_statements extension. If it fails or is weak: Tuning the slowest single query and ignoring the one that runs a million times. EXPLAIN Step What it does: EXPLAIN shows the plan the database chooses. If it "},"sql-ops/indexes-that-help-and-indexes-that-hurt":{"html":"\u003cp>An index is a trade. It makes some reads fast and makes every write slower and larger. Good index design means adding the few that matter and removing the ones that do not.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"what-you-will-learn\">What you will learn\u003c/h2>\n\u003cul>\n\u003cli>How a B-tree index is used, and why column order matters\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Composite, partial and covering indexes\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>The write cost of indexes and how to find unused ones\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Why an index is sometimes ignored\u003c/li>\n\u003c/ul>\n\u003ch2 id=\"how-a-b-tree-works-for-you\">How a B-tree works for you\u003c/h2>\n\u003cp>A B-tree index keeps values sorted. It answers equality, ranges and \u003ccode>ORDER BY\u003c/code> on the indexed columns. With a \u003cstrong>composite\u003c/strong> index on \u003ccode>(customer_id, created_at)\u003c/code>, rows are sorted by customer first, then by date inside each customer.\u003c/p>\n\u003cp>The rule of thumb for column order: columns tested for \u003cstrong>equality\u003c/strong> first, then the column used for a \u003cstrong>range or sort\u003c/strong>.\u003c/p>\n\u003cdiv class=\"code console\" data-lang=\"terminal\">\u003cbutton class=\"copy\" type=\"button\" data-copy=\"psql -c &quot;CREATE INDEX CONCURRENTLY idx_orders_cust_created ON orders (customer_id, created_at);&quot;\npsql -c &quot;EXPLAIN SELECT * FROM orders WHERE customer_id = 4711 AND created_at &gt;= '2026-10-01' ORDER BY created_at DESC LIMIT 20;&quot; | head -3\" aria-label=\"Copy commands\">Copy commands\u003c/button>\u003cpre tabindex=\"0\">\u003ccode class=\"hljs\">\u003cspan class=\"ln cmd\">\u003cspan class=\"ps\" aria-hidden=\"true\">$ \u003c/span>psql -c \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">&quot;CREATE INDEX CONCURRENTLY idx_orders_cust_created ON orders (customer_id, created_at);&quot;\u003c/span>\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln cmd\">\u003cspan class=\"ps\" aria-hidden=\"true\">$ \u003c/span>psql -c \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">&quot;EXPLAIN SELECT * FROM orders WHERE customer_id = 4711 AND created_at &gt;= &#x27;2026-10-01&#x27; ORDER BY created_at DESC LIMIT 20;&quot;\u003c/span> | \u003cspan class=\"hljs-built_in\">head\u003c/span> -3\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln out\"> Limit  (cost=0.57..38.10 rows=20 width=96)\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln out\">   -&gt;  Index Scan Backward using idx_orders_cust_created on orders\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln out\">         Index Cond: ((customer_id = 4711) AND (created_at &gt;= '2026-10-01'))\u003c/span>\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>\n\u003cp>This one index serves queries by customer alone, and by customer plus date, and also gives the sort for free. It does \u003cstrong>not\u003c/strong> help a query that filters only on \u003ccode>created_at\u003c/code>, because the sorted order begins with customer.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"partial-and-covering-indexes\">Partial and covering indexes\u003c/h2>\n\u003cp>A \u003cstrong>partial index\u003c/strong> covers only some rows. If 2 percent of orders are \u003ccode>status=&#39;open&#39;\u003c/code> and queries look only for those, a smaller index is faster to scan and cheaper to maintain.\u003c/p>\n\u003cdiv class=\"code\" data-lang=\"text\">\u003cbutton class=\"copy\" type=\"button\" data-copy=\"CREATE INDEX CONCURRENTLY idx_orders_open ON orders (customer_id) WHERE status = 'open';\" aria-label=\"Copy code\">Copy\u003c/button>\u003cpre tabindex=\"0\">\u003ccode class=\"hljs\">CREATE INDEX CONCURRENTLY idx_orders_open ON orders (customer_id) WHERE status = &#x27;open&#x27;;\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>\n\u003cp>A \u003cstrong>covering index\u003c/strong> adds extra columns so the database can answer from the index alone (an index-only scan):\u003c/p>\n\u003cdiv class=\"code\" data-lang=\"text\">\u003cbutton class=\"copy\" type=\"button\" data-copy=\"CREATE INDEX CONCURRENTLY idx_orders_cust_total ON orders (customer_id) INCLUDE (total);\" aria-label=\"Copy code\">Copy\u003c/button>\u003cpre tabindex=\"0\">\u003ccode class=\"hljs\">CREATE INDEX CONCURRENTLY idx_orders_cust_total ON orders (customer_id) INCLUDE (total);\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>\n\u003cp>Index-only scans also need the table&#39;s visibility map to be current, which vacuum keeps up to date.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"the-cost-of-indexes\">The cost of indexes\u003c/h2>\n\u003cp>Every insert updates every index on the table. Every update that changes an indexed column does too. Indexes also take disk and memory and slow vacuum. A table with twelve indexes may have write speed far below one with three.\u003c/p>\n\u003cp>Find indexes that are never used:\u003c/p>\n\u003cdiv class=\"code console\" data-lang=\"terminal\">\u003cbutton class=\"copy\" type=\"button\" data-copy=\"psql -c &quot;SELECT relname, indexrelname, idx_scan, pg_size_pretty(pg_relation_size(indexrelid)) AS size FROM pg_stat_user_indexes ORDER BY idx_scan, pg_relation_size(indexrelid) DESC LIMIT 3;&quot;\" aria-label=\"Copy commands\">Copy commands\u003c/button>\u003cpre tabindex=\"0\">\u003ccode class=\"hljs\">\u003cspan class=\"ln cmd\">\u003cspan class=\"ps\" aria-hidden=\"true\">$ \u003c/span>psql -c \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">&quot;SELECT relname, indexrelname, idx_scan, pg_size_pretty(pg_relation_size(indexrelid)) AS size FROM pg_stat_user_indexes ORDER BY idx_scan, pg_relation_size(indexrelid) DESC LIMIT 3;&quot;\u003c/span>\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln out\"> relname | indexrelname        | idx_scan | size\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln out\">---------+---------------------+----------+--------\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln out\"> orders  | idx_orders_status   |        0 | 1.8 GB\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln out\"> orders  | idx_orders_created  |       12 | 1.6 GB\u003c/span>\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>\n\u003cp>\u003ccode>idx_scan = 0\u003c/code> since the last statistics reset is a candidate for removal, but check first that the statistics cover a full business cycle (month end, reporting jobs) and that the index does not enforce a unique constraint. Drop with \u003ccode>DROP INDEX CONCURRENTLY\u003c/code>.\u003c/p>\n\u003cp>Duplicate indexes are common too: an index on \u003ccode>(customer_id)\u003c/code> is redundant when \u003ccode>(customer_id, created_at)\u003c/code> exists, because the composite serves the same lookups.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"when-the-planner-ignores-an-index\">When the planner ignores an index\u003c/h2>\n\u003cul>\n\u003cli>The query returns a large part of the table, so reading the table directly is cheaper.\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>A function wraps the column: \u003ccode>WHERE lower(email) = &#39;a@b.c&#39;\u003c/code> cannot use an index on \u003ccode>email\u003c/code>. Create an expression index on \u003ccode>lower(email)\u003c/code>, or change the query.\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>The types differ and force a cast of the column.\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Statistics are stale and the planner thinks the table is small.\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>A leading wildcard: \u003ccode>LIKE &#39;%smith&#39;\u003c/code> cannot use a plain B-tree.\u003c/li>\n\u003c/ul>\n\u003ch2 id=\"how-the-parts-fit-together\">How the parts fit together\u003c/h2>\n\u003cp>The lesson in one picture: each box is a section of this lesson, in the order you meet it. Select a box to see what it does and the mistake that usually goes with it.\u003c/p>\n\u003cfigure class=\"dg\" data-rows=\"2\" data-edges=\"n1&gt;n2;n2&gt;n3;n3&gt;n4\" style=\"--rows:2\">\u003cfigcaption class=\"dg-title\">How Indexes that help and indexes that hurt fits together\u003c/figcaption>\u003cdiv class=\"dg-stage\">\u003csvg viewBox=\"0 0 100 100\" preserveAspectRatio=\"none\" aria-hidden=\"true\" focusable=\"false\">\u003cline x1=\"12.5\" y1=\"25.0\" x2=\"37.5\" y2=\"75.0\"/>\u003cline x1=\"37.5\" y1=\"75.0\" x2=\"62.5\" y2=\"25.0\"/>\u003cline x1=\"62.5\" y1=\"25.0\" x2=\"87.5\" y2=\"75.0\"/>\u003c/svg>\u003cbutton type=\"button\" class=\"dg-node k-process\" data-act=\"dg-pick\" data-id=\"n1\" data-kind=\"process\" style=\"left:12.5%;top:25.0%;--w:22.0%\" aria-pressed=\"false\">\u003cspan class=\"dg-name\">How a B-tree works for you\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"dg-kind\">Step\u003c/span>\u003c/button>\u003cbutton type=\"button\" class=\"dg-node k-store\" data-act=\"dg-pick\" data-id=\"n2\" data-kind=\"store\" style=\"left:37.5%;top:75.0%;--w:22.0%\" aria-pressed=\"false\">\u003cspan class=\"dg-name\">Partial and covering indexes\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"dg-kind\">Data store\u003c/span>\u003c/button>\u003cbutton type=\"button\" class=\"dg-node k-store\" data-act=\"dg-pick\" data-id=\"n3\" data-kind=\"store\" style=\"left:62.5%;top:25.0%;--w:22.0%\" aria-pressed=\"false\">\u003cspan class=\"dg-name\">The cost of indexes\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"dg-kind\">Data store\u003c/span>\u003c/button>\u003cbutton type=\"button\" class=\"dg-node k-store\" data-act=\"dg-pick\" data-id=\"n4\" data-kind=\"store\" style=\"left:87.5%;top:75.0%;--w:22.0%\" aria-pressed=\"false\">\u003cspan class=\"dg-name\">When the planner ignores an index\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"dg-kind\">Data store\u003c/span>\u003c/button>\u003c/div>\u003cp class=\"sr\">Flow in order: 1, How a B-tree works for you to Partial and covering indexes; 2, Partial and covering indexes to The cost of indexes; 3, The cost of indexes to When the planner ignores an index.\u003c/p>\u003cul class=\"dg-legend\" aria-label=\"Legend\">\u003cli>\u003ci class=\"dg-sw k-process\" aria-hidden=\"true\">\u003c/i>Step\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003ci class=\"dg-sw k-store\" aria-hidden=\"true\">\u003c/i>Data store\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003ci class=\"dg-arrow\" aria-hidden=\"true\">\u003c/i>Numbered arrows show the order of the flow\u003c/li>\u003c/ul>\u003cp class=\"dg-hint small\">Select a component to see what it does and what happens when it fails. \u003cbutton type=\"button\" class=\"btn btn-quiet dg-flow-btn\" data-act=\"dg-flow\" aria-pressed=\"false\">Show the flow\u003c/button>\u003c/p>\u003cdiv class=\"dg-live\" role=\"status\" aria-live=\"polite\">\u003c/div>\u003cdl class=\"dg-list\">\u003cdiv class=\"dg-item\" data-id=\"n1\">\u003cdt>How a B-tree works for you \u003cspan class=\"dg-kt\">Step\u003c/span>\u003c/dt>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">What it does:\u003c/span> A B-tree index keeps values sorted. It answers equality, ranges and ORDER BY on the indexed columns.\u003c/dd>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">If it fails or is weak:\u003c/span> Putting the range column before the equality column in a composite index.\u003c/dd>\u003c/div>\u003cdiv class=\"dg-item\" data-id=\"n2\">\u003cdt>Partial and covering indexes \u003cspan class=\"dg-kt\">Data store\u003c/span>\u003c/dt>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">What it does:\u003c/span> A partial index covers only some rows. If 2 percent of orders are status='open' and queries look only for those, a smaller index is faster to scan and cheaper to...\u003c/dd>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">If it fails or is weak:\u003c/span> Adding an index for every slow query without checking existing ones.\u003c/dd>\u003c/div>\u003cdiv class=\"dg-item\" data-id=\"n3\">\u003cdt>The cost of indexes \u003cspan class=\"dg-kt\">Data store\u003c/span>\u003c/dt>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">What it does:\u003c/span> Every insert updates every index on the table.\u003c/dd>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">If it fails or is weak:\u003c/span> Dropping an index that backs a unique or foreign key constraint.\u003c/dd>\u003c/div>\u003cdiv class=\"dg-item\" data-id=\"n4\">\u003cdt>When the planner ignores an index \u003cspan class=\"dg-kt\">Data store\u003c/span>\u003c/dt>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">What it does:\u003c/span> The query returns a large part of the table, so reading the table directly is cheaper.\u003c/dd>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">If it fails or is weak:\u003c/span> Wrapping an indexed column in a function.\u003c/dd>\u003c/div>\u003c/dl>\u003c/figure>\n\u003ch2 id=\"incident-twenty-indexes-and-a-slow-import\">Incident: Twenty indexes and a slow import\u003c/h2>\n\u003caside class=\"callout callout-incident\">\u003cdiv class=\"callout-title\">Incident\u003c/div>\u003cp>A nightly import that took 20 minutes grew to 5 hours over a year.\u003c/p>\n\u003c/aside>\n\u003cdiv class=\"code console\" data-lang=\"terminal\">\u003cbutton class=\"copy\" type=\"button\" data-copy=\"psql -c &quot;SELECT count(*) FROM pg_indexes WHERE tablename='events';&quot;\npsql -c &quot;SELECT pg_size_pretty(pg_table_size('events')) AS table, pg_size_pretty(pg_indexes_size('events')) AS indexes;&quot;\" aria-label=\"Copy commands\">Copy commands\u003c/button>\u003cpre tabindex=\"0\">\u003ccode class=\"hljs\">\u003cspan class=\"ln cmd\">\u003cspan class=\"ps\" aria-hidden=\"true\">$ \u003c/span>psql -c \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">&quot;SELECT count(*) FROM pg_indexes WHERE tablename=&#x27;events&#x27;;&quot;\u003c/span>\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln out\"> count\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln out\">-------\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln out\">    23\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln cmd\">\u003cspan class=\"ps\" aria-hidden=\"true\">$ \u003c/span>psql -c \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">&quot;SELECT pg_size_pretty(pg_table_size(&#x27;events&#x27;)) AS table, pg_size_pretty(pg_indexes_size(&#x27;events&#x27;)) AS indexes;&quot;\u003c/span>\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln out\"> table  | indexes\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln out\">--------+---------\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln out\"> 41 GB  | 87 GB\u003c/span>\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>\n\u003cp>Over time people added an index for each new report. The import had to update 23 indexes for every row, and the indexes were twice the size of the table. A review found nine unused and four duplicate indexes. Removing thirteen brought the import back under an hour. Each index had been a reasonable choice when it was added; nobody owned the total.\u003c/p>\n\u003csection class=\"wp\" aria-label=\"Worked example with a wrong path\">\u003ch4 class=\"wp-h\">Worked example: a tempting wrong path\u003c/h4>\u003cp class=\"wp-goal\">\u003cstrong>Goal.\u003c/strong> The query is slow, so index every column in the WHERE clause.\u003c/p>\u003cdiv class=\"wp-step wp-bad\">\u003cp class=\"wp-tag\">The tempting way\u003c/p>\u003cpre tabindex=\"0\">\u003ccode>CREATE INDEX ON orders (status);\nCREATE INDEX ON orders (created_at);\nCREATE INDEX ON orders (customer_id);\nCREATE INDEX ON orders (total);\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>\u003cdetails class=\"wp-more\">\u003csummary>See what goes wrong\u003c/summary>\u003cpre tabindex=\"0\">\u003ccode>(queries are a bit faster)\n$ \\di+ orders*\n4 indexes, 18 GB\ninserts: 1200/s down to 400/s\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003cp class=\"wp-why\">\u003cstrong>Why it fails.\u003c/strong> Each index must be updated on every write. A low-selectivity index on status is never used, and the planner often ignores single-column indexes when a composite one would fit the query.\u003c/p>\u003c/details>\u003cdetails class=\"wp-more\">\u003csummary>See the way that works\u003c/summary>\u003cdiv class=\"wp-step wp-good\">\u003cp class=\"wp-tag\">The way that works\u003c/p>\u003cpre tabindex=\"0\">\u003ccode>EXPLAIN (ANALYZE, BUFFERS)\nSELECT * FROM orders WHERE customer_id = 4821 AND status = 'open' ORDER BY created_at DESC LIMIT 20;\nCREATE INDEX CONCURRENTLY ON orders (customer_id, created_at DESC) WHERE status = 'open';\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003cp>Read the plan, design one index for the real query, and drop indexes that are never used.\u003c/p>\u003c/div>\u003c/details>\u003c/section>\n\u003ch2 id=\"common-mistakes\">Common mistakes\u003c/h2>\n\u003cul>\n\u003cli>Adding an index for every slow query without checking existing ones\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Putting the range column before the equality column in a composite index\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Wrapping an indexed column in a function\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Dropping an index that backs a unique or foreign key constraint\u003c/li>\n\u003c/ul>\n","toc":[{"id":"what-you-will-learn","text":"What you will learn"},{"id":"how-a-b-tree-works-for-you","text":"How a B-tree works for you"},{"id":"partial-and-covering-indexes","text":"Partial and covering indexes"},{"id":"the-cost-of-indexes","text":"The cost of indexes"},{"id":"when-the-planner-ignores-an-index","text":"When the planner ignores an index"},{"id":"how-the-parts-fit-together","text":"How the parts fit together"},{"id":"incident-twenty-indexes-and-a-slow-import","text":"Incident: Twenty indexes and a slow import"},{"id":"common-mistakes","text":"Common mistakes"}],"refs":[{"t":"PostgreSQL: Indexes","u":"https://www.postgresql.org/docs/current/indexes.html"},{"t":"PostgreSQL: Multicolumn indexes","u":"https://www.postgresql.org/docs/current/indexes-multicolumn.html"},{"t":"Use The Index, Luke","u":"https://use-the-index-luke.com/"}],"text":"An index is a trade. It makes some reads fast and makes every write slower and larger. Good index design means adding the few that matter and removing the ones that do not. What you will learn How a B-tree index is used, and why column order matters Composite, partial and covering indexes The write cost of indexes and how to find unused ones Why an index is sometimes ignored How a B-tree works for you A B-tree index keeps values sorted. It answers equality, ranges and ORDER BY on the indexed columns. With a composite index on (customer_id, created_at) , rows are sorted by customer first, then by date inside each customer. The rule of thumb for column order: columns tested for equality first, then the column used for a range or sort . $ psql -c \"CREATE INDEX CONCURRENTLY idx_orders_cust_created ON orders (customer_id, created_at);\" $ psql -c \"EXPLAIN SELECT * FROM orders WHERE customer_id = 4711 AND created_at >= &#x27;2026-10-01&#x27; ORDER BY created_at DESC LIMIT 20;\" | head -3 Limit (cost=0.57..38.10 rows=20 width=96) -> Index Scan Backward using idx_orders_cust_created on orders Index Cond: ((customer_id = 4711) AND (created_at >= '2026-10-01')) This one index serves queries by customer alone, and by customer plus date, and also gives the sort for free. It does not help a query that filters only on created_at , because the sorted order begins with customer. Partial and covering indexes A partial index covers only some rows. If 2 percent of orders are status=&#39;open&#39; and queries look only for those, a smaller index is faster to scan and cheaper to maintain. CREATE INDEX CONCURRENTLY idx_orders_open ON orders (customer_id) WHERE status = &#x27;open&#x27;; A covering index adds extra columns so the database can answer from the index alone (an index-only scan): CREATE INDEX CONCURRENTLY idx_orders_cust_total ON orders (customer_id) INCLUDE (total); Index-only scans also need the table&#39;s visibility map to be current, which vacuum keeps up to date. The cost of indexes Every insert updates every index on the table. Every update that changes an indexed column does too. Indexes also take disk and memory and slow vacuum. A table with twelve indexes may have write speed far below one with three. Find indexes that are never used: $ psql -c \"SELECT relname, indexrelname, idx_scan, pg_size_pretty(pg_relation_size(indexrelid)) AS size FROM pg_stat_user_indexes ORDER BY idx_scan, pg_relation_size(indexrelid) DESC LIMIT 3;\" relname | indexrelname | idx_scan | size ---------+---------------------+----------+-------- orders | idx_orders_status | 0 | 1.8 GB orders | idx_orders_created | 12 | 1.6 GB idx_scan = 0 since the last statistics reset is a candidate for removal, but check first that the statistics cover a full business cycle (month end, reporting jobs) and that the index does not enforce a unique constraint. Drop with DROP INDEX CONCURRENTLY . Duplicate indexes are common too: an index on (customer_id) is redundant when (customer_id, created_at) exists, because the composite serves the same lookups. When the planner ignores an index The query returns a large part of the table, so reading the table directly is cheaper. A function wraps the column: WHERE lower(email) = &#39;a@b.c&#39; cannot use an index on email . Create an expression index on lower(email) , or change the query. The types differ and force a cast of the column. Statistics are stale and the planner thinks the table is small. A leading wildcard: LIKE &#39;%smith&#39; cannot use a plain B-tree. How the parts fit together The lesson in one picture: each box is a section of this lesson, in the order you meet it. Select a box to see what it does and the mistake that usually goes with it. How Indexes that help and indexes that hurt fits together Flow in order: 1, How a B-tree works for you to Partial and covering indexes; 2, Partial and covering indexes to The cost of indexes; 3, The cost of indexes to When the planner ignores an index. Step Data store Numbered arrow"},"sql-ops/locks-deadlocks-and-long-transactions":{"html":"\u003cp>A query can be slow because it is working, or because it is waiting. Locks are the usual reason for waiting, and one forgotten transaction can stop a whole application.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"what-you-will-learn\">What you will learn\u003c/h2>\n\u003cul>\n\u003cli>Row locks, table locks and how they conflict\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Finding the blocker with pg_stat_activity and pg_blocking_pids\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Idle in transaction and why it is dangerous\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Deadlocks and the timeouts that limit damage\u003c/li>\n\u003c/ul>\n\u003ch2 id=\"locks-in-short\">Locks in short\u003c/h2>\n\u003cp>When a transaction updates a row, it holds a lock on it until the transaction ends. Other writers to that row wait. Readers are not blocked, because PostgreSQL keeps old row versions for them (MVCC). Statements such as \u003ccode>ALTER TABLE\u003c/code> take stronger table-level locks, and those can queue behind a long reader and then block everyone behind them.\u003c/p>\n\u003cp>The key sentence: \u003cstrong>locks are held until COMMIT or ROLLBACK\u003c/strong>, not until the statement ends.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"find-the-blocker\">Find the blocker\u003c/h2>\n\u003cdiv class=\"code console\" data-lang=\"terminal\">\u003cbutton class=\"copy\" type=\"button\" data-copy=\"psql -c &quot;SELECT pid, state, wait_event_type, wait_event, now()-xact_start AS tx_age, left(query,50) AS query FROM pg_stat_activity WHERE datname='shop' ORDER BY xact_start;&quot;\npsql -c &quot;SELECT pid, pg_blocking_pids(pid) AS blocked_by FROM pg_stat_activity WHERE cardinality(pg_blocking_pids(pid)) &gt; 0;&quot;\" aria-label=\"Copy commands\">Copy commands\u003c/button>\u003cpre tabindex=\"0\">\u003ccode class=\"hljs\">\u003cspan class=\"ln cmd\">\u003cspan class=\"ps\" aria-hidden=\"true\">$ \u003c/span>psql -c \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">&quot;SELECT pid, state, wait_event_type, wait_event, now()-xact_start AS tx_age, left(query,50) AS query FROM pg_stat_activity WHERE datname=&#x27;shop&#x27; ORDER BY xact_start;&quot;\u003c/span>\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln out\"> pid  | state               | wait_event_type | wait_event    | tx_age   | query\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln out\">------+---------------------+-----------------+---------------+----------+------------------------------------------------\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln out\"> 2210 | idle in transaction |                 |               | 03:12:44 | UPDATE accounts SET balance = balance - 10 WHERE\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln out\"> 2488 | active              | Lock            | transactionid | 00:41:02 | UPDATE accounts SET balance = balance + 10 WHERE\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln cmd\">\u003cspan class=\"ps\" aria-hidden=\"true\">$ \u003c/span>psql -c \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">&quot;SELECT pid, pg_blocking_pids(pid) AS blocked_by FROM pg_stat_activity WHERE cardinality(pg_blocking_pids(pid)) &gt; 0;&quot;\u003c/span>\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln out\"> pid  | blocked_by\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln out\">------+------------\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln out\"> 2488 | {2210}\u003c/span>\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>\n\u003cp>Process 2488 waits for a lock held by 2210. Process 2210 is \u003cstrong>idle in transaction\u003c/strong>: it started a transaction, did an update, and then did nothing for three hours. The client opened a transaction and never finished it, often because of a bug, a crashed worker, or someone&#39;s session left open in a terminal.\u003c/p>\n\u003cp>You can stop the blocker with \u003ccode>SELECT pg_terminate_backend(2210);\u003c/code>. That rolls back its work, so ask who owns it if the data matters. \u003ccode>pg_cancel_backend(pid)\u003c/code> cancels only the running statement and is gentler.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"long-transactions-hurt-in-other-ways\">Long transactions hurt in other ways\u003c/h2>\n\u003cp>A transaction that stays open holds back the cleanup of old row versions across the database, so tables grow and vacuum cannot remove dead rows. It also holds locks and may keep a replica in conflict. Keep transactions short, and never keep one open while waiting for a person or a slow outside call.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"deadlocks\">Deadlocks\u003c/h2>\n\u003cp>A \u003cstrong>deadlock\u003c/strong> happens when two transactions each hold a lock the other needs. PostgreSQL detects it after \u003ccode>deadlock_timeout\u003c/code> (1 second by default), aborts one of them with an error, and lets the other continue.\u003c/p>\n\u003cdiv class=\"code console\" data-lang=\"terminal\">\u003cpre tabindex=\"0\">\u003ccode class=\"hljs\">\u003cspan class=\"ln out\">ERROR:  deadlock detected\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln out\">DETAIL:  Process 2210 waits for ShareLock on transaction 8841; blocked by process 2488.\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln out\">         Process 2488 waits for ShareLock on transaction 8839; blocked by process 2210.\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln out\">HINT:  See server log for query details.\u003c/span>\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>\n\u003cp>The cure is in the application: touch rows in the same order everywhere (for example, always update the account with the lower id first), keep transactions short, and retry the failed transaction.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"timeouts-that-protect-you\">Timeouts that protect you\u003c/h2>\n\u003cul>\n\u003cli>\u003ccode>lock_timeout\u003c/code>: how long a statement waits for a lock before giving up. Set it for migrations, so an \u003ccode>ALTER TABLE\u003c/code> that cannot get its lock fails quickly instead of queueing behind a reader and freezing the table.\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>\u003ccode>idle_in_transaction_session_timeout\u003c/code>: ends sessions that sit in a transaction doing nothing.\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>\u003ccode>statement_timeout\u003c/code>: caps how long a single statement may run.\u003c/li>\n\u003c/ul>\n\u003cdiv class=\"code\" data-lang=\"text\">\u003cbutton class=\"copy\" type=\"button\" data-copy=\"ALTER ROLE app SET idle_in_transaction_session_timeout = '60s';\nALTER ROLE app SET statement_timeout = '30s';\" aria-label=\"Copy code\">Copy\u003c/button>\u003cpre tabindex=\"0\">\u003ccode class=\"hljs\">ALTER ROLE app SET idle_in_transaction_session_timeout = &#x27;60s&#x27;;\nALTER ROLE app SET statement_timeout = &#x27;30s&#x27;;\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>\n\u003ch2 id=\"how-the-parts-fit-together\">How the parts fit together\u003c/h2>\n\u003cp>The lesson in one picture: each box is a section of this lesson, in the order you meet it. Select a box to see what it does and the mistake that usually goes with it.\u003c/p>\n\u003cfigure class=\"dg\" data-rows=\"2\" data-edges=\"n1&gt;n2;n2&gt;n3;n3&gt;n4;n4&gt;n5\" style=\"--rows:2\">\u003cfigcaption class=\"dg-title\">How Locks, deadlocks and long transactions fits together\u003c/figcaption>\u003cdiv class=\"dg-stage\">\u003csvg viewBox=\"0 0 100 100\" preserveAspectRatio=\"none\" aria-hidden=\"true\" focusable=\"false\">\u003cline x1=\"10.0\" y1=\"25.0\" x2=\"30.0\" y2=\"75.0\"/>\u003cline x1=\"30.0\" y1=\"75.0\" x2=\"50.0\" y2=\"25.0\"/>\u003cline x1=\"50.0\" y1=\"25.0\" x2=\"70.0\" y2=\"75.0\"/>\u003cline x1=\"70.0\" y1=\"75.0\" x2=\"90.0\" y2=\"25.0\"/>\u003c/svg>\u003cbutton type=\"button\" class=\"dg-node k-process\" data-act=\"dg-pick\" data-id=\"n1\" data-kind=\"process\" style=\"left:10.0%;top:25.0%;--w:17.6%\" aria-pressed=\"false\">\u003cspan class=\"dg-name\">Locks in short\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"dg-kind\">Step\u003c/span>\u003c/button>\u003cbutton type=\"button\" class=\"dg-node k-process\" data-act=\"dg-pick\" data-id=\"n2\" data-kind=\"process\" style=\"left:30.0%;top:75.0%;--w:17.6%\" aria-pressed=\"false\">\u003cspan class=\"dg-name\">Find the blocker\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"dg-kind\">Step\u003c/span>\u003c/button>\u003cbutton type=\"button\" class=\"dg-node k-process\" data-act=\"dg-pick\" data-id=\"n3\" data-kind=\"process\" style=\"left:50.0%;top:25.0%;--w:17.6%\" aria-pressed=\"false\">\u003cspan class=\"dg-name\">Long transactions hurt in other ways\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"dg-kind\">Step\u003c/span>\u003c/button>\u003cbutton type=\"button\" class=\"dg-node k-process\" data-act=\"dg-pick\" data-id=\"n4\" data-kind=\"process\" style=\"left:70.0%;top:75.0%;--w:17.6%\" aria-pressed=\"false\">\u003cspan class=\"dg-name\">Deadlocks\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"dg-kind\">Step\u003c/span>\u003c/button>\u003cbutton type=\"button\" class=\"dg-node k-process\" data-act=\"dg-pick\" data-id=\"n5\" data-kind=\"process\" style=\"left:90.0%;top:25.0%;--w:17.6%\" aria-pressed=\"false\">\u003cspan class=\"dg-name\">Timeouts that protect you\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"dg-kind\">Step\u003c/span>\u003c/button>\u003c/div>\u003cp class=\"sr\">Flow in order: 1, Locks in short to Find the blocker; 2, Find the blocker to Long transactions hurt in other ways; 3, Long transactions hurt in other ways to Deadlocks; 4, Deadlocks to Timeouts that protect you.\u003c/p>\u003cul class=\"dg-legend\" aria-label=\"Legend\">\u003cli>\u003ci class=\"dg-sw k-process\" aria-hidden=\"true\">\u003c/i>Step\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003ci class=\"dg-arrow\" aria-hidden=\"true\">\u003c/i>Numbered arrows show the order of the flow\u003c/li>\u003c/ul>\u003cp class=\"dg-hint small\">Select a component to see what it does and what happens when it fails. \u003cbutton type=\"button\" class=\"btn btn-quiet dg-flow-btn\" data-act=\"dg-flow\" aria-pressed=\"false\">Show the flow\u003c/button>\u003c/p>\u003cdiv class=\"dg-live\" role=\"status\" aria-live=\"polite\">\u003c/div>\u003cdl class=\"dg-list\">\u003cdiv class=\"dg-item\" data-id=\"n1\">\u003cdt>Locks in short \u003cspan class=\"dg-kt\">Step\u003c/span>\u003c/dt>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">What it does:\u003c/span> When a transaction updates a row, it holds a lock on it until the transaction ends.\u003c/dd>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">If it fails or is weak:\u003c/span> Running migrations with no lock_timeout.\u003c/dd>\u003c/div>\u003cdiv class=\"dg-item\" data-id=\"n2\">\u003cdt>Find the blocker \u003cspan class=\"dg-kt\">Step\u003c/span>\u003c/dt>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">What it does:\u003c/span> Process 2488 waits for a lock held by 2210.\u003c/dd>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">If it fails or is weak:\u003c/span> Ignoring deadlock errors instead of fixing lock order and retrying.\u003c/dd>\u003c/div>\u003cdiv class=\"dg-item\" data-id=\"n3\">\u003cdt>Long transactions hurt in other ways \u003cspan class=\"dg-kt\">Step\u003c/span>\u003c/dt>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">What it does:\u003c/span> A transaction that stays open holds back the cleanup of old row versions across the database, so tables grow and vacuum cannot remove dead rows.\u003c/dd>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">If it fails or is weak:\u003c/span> Leaving transactions open while waiting for user input or HTTP calls.\u003c/dd>\u003c/div>\u003cdiv class=\"dg-item\" data-id=\"n4\">\u003cdt>Deadlocks \u003cspan class=\"dg-kt\">Step\u003c/span>\u003c/dt>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">What it does:\u003c/span> A deadlock happens when two transactions each hold a lock the other needs.\u003c/dd>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">If it fails or is weak:\u003c/span> Check the Common mistakes list below this diagram for what usually goes wrong here.\u003c/dd>\u003c/div>\u003cdiv class=\"dg-item\" data-id=\"n5\">\u003cdt>Timeouts that protect you \u003cspan class=\"dg-kt\">Step\u003c/span>\u003c/dt>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">What it does:\u003c/span> lock_timeout: how long a statement waits for a lock before giving up.\u003c/dd>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">If it fails or is weak:\u003c/span> Killing the victim instead of the blocker.\u003c/dd>\u003c/div>\u003c/dl>\u003c/figure>\n\u003ch2 id=\"incident-the-migration-that-froze-checkout\">Incident: The migration that froze checkout\u003c/h2>\n\u003caside class=\"callout callout-incident\">\u003cdiv class=\"callout-title\">Incident\u003c/div>\u003cp>A one-line schema change brought checkout to a halt for four minutes.\u003c/p>\n\u003c/aside>\n\u003cdiv class=\"code console\" data-lang=\"terminal\">\u003cbutton class=\"copy\" type=\"button\" data-copy=\"psql -c &quot;SELECT pid, state, wait_event_type, now()-query_start AS age, left(query,45) FROM pg_stat_activity WHERE state &lt;&gt; 'idle' ORDER BY query_start LIMIT 3;&quot;\" aria-label=\"Copy commands\">Copy commands\u003c/button>\u003cpre tabindex=\"0\">\u003ccode class=\"hljs\">\u003cspan class=\"ln cmd\">\u003cspan class=\"ps\" aria-hidden=\"true\">$ \u003c/span>psql -c \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">&quot;SELECT pid, state, wait_event_type, now()-query_start AS age, left(query,45) FROM pg_stat_activity WHERE state &lt;&gt; &#x27;idle&#x27; ORDER BY query_start LIMIT 3;&quot;\u003c/span>\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln out\"> pid  | state  | wait_event_type | age      | left\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln out\">------+--------+-----------------+----------+---------------------------------------------\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln out\"> 1190 | active |                 | 00:04:10 | SELECT count(*) FROM orders  -- report\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln out\"> 1304 | active | Lock            | 00:03:55 | ALTER TABLE orders ADD COLUMN note text\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln out\"> 1350 | active | Lock            | 00:03:50 | INSERT INTO orders (customer_id, total)\u003c/span>\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>\n\u003cp>A long report held a weak lock on \u003ccode>orders\u003c/code>. The \u003ccode>ALTER TABLE\u003c/code> needed a strong lock and queued behind it. Every new insert then queued behind the \u003ccode>ALTER\u003c/code>, so checkout stopped even though the report only read data. Setting \u003ccode>lock_timeout = &#39;3s&#39;\u003c/code> for the migration lets it fail fast and be retried in a quiet moment, and the report was moved to a replica.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"common-mistakes\">Common mistakes\u003c/h2>\n\u003cul>\n\u003cli>Running migrations with no lock_timeout\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Leaving transactions open while waiting for user input or HTTP calls\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Killing the victim instead of the blocker\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Ignoring deadlock errors instead of fixing lock order and retrying\u003c/li>\n\u003c/ul>\n","toc":[{"id":"what-you-will-learn","text":"What you will learn"},{"id":"locks-in-short","text":"Locks in short"},{"id":"find-the-blocker","text":"Find the blocker"},{"id":"long-transactions-hurt-in-other-ways","text":"Long transactions hurt in other ways"},{"id":"deadlocks","text":"Deadlocks"},{"id":"timeouts-that-protect-you","text":"Timeouts that protect you"},{"id":"how-the-parts-fit-together","text":"How the parts fit together"},{"id":"incident-the-migration-that-froze-checkout","text":"Incident: The migration that froze checkout"},{"id":"common-mistakes","text":"Common mistakes"}],"refs":[{"t":"PostgreSQL: Explicit locking","u":"https://www.postgresql.org/docs/current/explicit-locking.html"},{"t":"PostgreSQL: Monitoring activity","u":"https://www.postgresql.org/docs/current/monitoring-stats.html"},{"t":"PostgreSQL: Server configuration, client connection defaults","u":"https://www.postgresql.org/docs/current/runtime-config-client.html"}],"text":"A query can be slow because it is working, or because it is waiting. Locks are the usual reason for waiting, and one forgotten transaction can stop a whole application. What you will learn Row locks, table locks and how they conflict Finding the blocker with pg_stat_activity and pg_blocking_pids Idle in transaction and why it is dangerous Deadlocks and the timeouts that limit damage Locks in short When a transaction updates a row, it holds a lock on it until the transaction ends. Other writers to that row wait. Readers are not blocked, because PostgreSQL keeps old row versions for them (MVCC). Statements such as ALTER TABLE take stronger table-level locks, and those can queue behind a long reader and then block everyone behind them. The key sentence: locks are held until COMMIT or ROLLBACK , not until the statement ends. Find the blocker $ psql -c \"SELECT pid, state, wait_event_type, wait_event, now()-xact_start AS tx_age, left(query,50) AS query FROM pg_stat_activity WHERE datname=&#x27;shop&#x27; ORDER BY xact_start;\" pid | state | wait_event_type | wait_event | tx_age | query ------+---------------------+-----------------+---------------+----------+------------------------------------------------ 2210 | idle in transaction | | | 03:12:44 | UPDATE accounts SET balance = balance - 10 WHERE 2488 | active | Lock | transactionid | 00:41:02 | UPDATE accounts SET balance = balance + 10 WHERE $ psql -c \"SELECT pid, pg_blocking_pids(pid) AS blocked_by FROM pg_stat_activity WHERE cardinality(pg_blocking_pids(pid)) > 0;\" pid | blocked_by ------+------------ 2488 | {2210} Process 2488 waits for a lock held by 2210. Process 2210 is idle in transaction : it started a transaction, did an update, and then did nothing for three hours. The client opened a transaction and never finished it, often because of a bug, a crashed worker, or someone&#39;s session left open in a terminal. You can stop the blocker with SELECT pg_terminate_backend(2210); . That rolls back its work, so ask who owns it if the data matters. pg_cancel_backend(pid) cancels only the running statement and is gentler. Long transactions hurt in other ways A transaction that stays open holds back the cleanup of old row versions across the database, so tables grow and vacuum cannot remove dead rows. It also holds locks and may keep a replica in conflict. Keep transactions short, and never keep one open while waiting for a person or a slow outside call. Deadlocks A deadlock happens when two transactions each hold a lock the other needs. PostgreSQL detects it after deadlock_timeout (1 second by default), aborts one of them with an error, and lets the other continue. ERROR: deadlock detected DETAIL: Process 2210 waits for ShareLock on transaction 8841; blocked by process 2488. Process 2488 waits for ShareLock on transaction 8839; blocked by process 2210. HINT: See server log for query details. The cure is in the application: touch rows in the same order everywhere (for example, always update the account with the lower id first), keep transactions short, and retry the failed transaction. Timeouts that protect you lock_timeout : how long a statement waits for a lock before giving up. Set it for migrations, so an ALTER TABLE that cannot get its lock fails quickly instead of queueing behind a reader and freezing the table. idle_in_transaction_session_timeout : ends sessions that sit in a transaction doing nothing. statement_timeout : caps how long a single statement may run. ALTER ROLE app SET idle_in_transaction_session_timeout = &#x27;60s&#x27;; ALTER ROLE app SET statement_timeout = &#x27;30s&#x27;; How the parts fit together The lesson in one picture: each box is a section of this lesson, in the order you meet it. Select a box to see what it does and the mistake that usually goes with it. How Locks, deadlocks and long transactions fits together Flow in order: 1, Locks in short to Find the blocker; 2, Find the blocker to Long transactions hurt in other ways; 3, Long transactions hurt"},"sql-ops/connections-pooling-and-capacity":{"html":"\u003cp>A database does not slow down smoothly when it is overloaded. It runs fine, then it runs out of connections or memory, and fails quickly. The usual cause is that every application instance opens its own pool of connections.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"what-you-will-learn\">What you will learn\u003c/h2>\n\u003cul>\n\u003cli>Why PostgreSQL connections are expensive\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Connection pool maths for an application fleet\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>PgBouncer and its pooling modes\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Memory settings: shared_buffers, work_mem and their traps\u003c/li>\n\u003c/ul>\n\u003ch2 id=\"why-connections-cost\">Why connections cost\u003c/h2>\n\u003cp>PostgreSQL starts a separate process for each client connection. Each one uses memory and a slot in shared structures, and having hundreds of active sessions makes the CPUs spend time switching between them. A server with 16 cores often performs best with a few dozen running queries, not several hundred.\u003c/p>\n\u003cdiv class=\"code console\" data-lang=\"terminal\">\u003cbutton class=\"copy\" type=\"button\" data-copy=\"psql -c &quot;SHOW max_connections;&quot;\npsql -c &quot;SELECT state, count(*) FROM pg_stat_activity GROUP BY state;&quot;\" aria-label=\"Copy commands\">Copy commands\u003c/button>\u003cpre tabindex=\"0\">\u003ccode class=\"hljs\">\u003cspan class=\"ln cmd\">\u003cspan class=\"ps\" aria-hidden=\"true\">$ \u003c/span>psql -c \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">&quot;SHOW max_connections;&quot;\u003c/span>\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln out\"> max_connections\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln out\">-----------------\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln out\"> 200\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln cmd\">\u003cspan class=\"ps\" aria-hidden=\"true\">$ \u003c/span>psql -c \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">&quot;SELECT state, count(*) FROM pg_stat_activity GROUP BY state;&quot;\u003c/span>\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln out\"> state                | count\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln out\">----------------------+-------\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln out\"> active               |    14\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln out\"> idle                 |   171\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln out\"> idle in transaction  |     9\u003c/span>\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>\n\u003cp>Most of the 194 sessions are idle. They are still using memory and taking slots. When the limit is reached, new clients get:\u003c/p>\n\u003cdiv class=\"code console\" data-lang=\"terminal\">\u003cpre tabindex=\"0\">\u003ccode class=\"hljs\">\u003cspan class=\"ln out\">FATAL:  sorry, too many clients already\u003c/span>\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>\n\u003ch2 id=\"pool-maths\">Pool maths\u003c/h2>\n\u003cp>Each application process has its own pool. With 40 pods and a pool of 10 connections each, the application can open 400 connections, which is more than the database allows. Add a second deployment, a cron job and a reporting tool and the limit is crossed with no one noticing, until a deploy restarts all pods at once and every pod reconnects together.\u003c/p>\n\u003cp>Work out the total: \u003cstrong>instances × pool size\u003c/strong> across every client, and keep it below \u003ccode>max_connections\u003c/code> minus the reserved slots for administrators (\u003ccode>superuser_reserved_connections\u003c/code>, 3 by default).\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"pgbouncer\">PgBouncer\u003c/h2>\n\u003cp>A \u003cstrong>pooler\u003c/strong> sits between applications and the database. Applications connect to it by the thousands, and it keeps a small number of real connections.\u003c/p>\n\u003cul>\n\u003cli>\u003cstrong>Session pooling\u003c/strong>: a server connection is assigned for the life of the client connection. Safe, but saves little if clients stay connected.\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>\u003cstrong>Transaction pooling\u003c/strong>: a server connection is assigned only during a transaction. This is the most useful mode, and it limits what the application may use: session-level features such as \u003ccode>SET\u003c/code>, advisory locks, \u003ccode>LISTEN\u003c/code> and (in older versions) prepared statements may not behave as expected.\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>\u003cstrong>Statement pooling\u003c/strong>: one statement at a time. Rarely usable, as it forbids multi-statement transactions.\u003c/li>\n\u003c/ul>\n\u003cdiv class=\"code\" data-lang=\"text\">\u003cbutton class=\"copy\" type=\"button\" data-copy=\"[databases]\nshop = host=10.0.2.10 port=5432 dbname=shop\n\n[pgbouncer]\npool_mode = transaction\nmax_client_conn = 2000\ndefault_pool_size = 20\" aria-label=\"Copy code\">Copy\u003c/button>\u003cpre tabindex=\"0\">\u003ccode class=\"hljs\">[databases]\nshop = host=10.0.2.10 port=5432 dbname=shop\n\n[pgbouncer]\npool_mode = transaction\nmax_client_conn = 2000\ndefault_pool_size = 20\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>\n\u003cp>With these settings 2 000 clients can connect and at most 20 server connections per database and user pair are used.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"memory-settings\">Memory settings\u003c/h2>\n\u003cul>\n\u003cli>\u003cstrong>shared_buffers\u003c/strong>: the shared cache. A common starting point is about a quarter of RAM on a dedicated server.\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>\u003cstrong>work_mem\u003c/strong>: memory for \u003cstrong>each\u003c/strong> sort or hash step in \u003cstrong>each\u003c/strong> query. It is not per connection but per operation, so a complex query can use several times this value, and 200 connections doing so can add up to many gigabytes. Raising it globally is risky; raise it per role or per session for the reporting job.\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>\u003cstrong>maintenance_work_mem\u003c/strong>: used by vacuum and index builds.\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>\u003cstrong>effective_cache_size\u003c/strong>: tells the planner how much the OS is likely to cache. It allocates nothing.\u003c/li>\n\u003c/ul>\n\u003ch2 id=\"how-the-parts-fit-together\">How the parts fit together\u003c/h2>\n\u003cp>The lesson in one picture: each box is a section of this lesson, in the order you meet it. Select a box to see what it does and the mistake that usually goes with it.\u003c/p>\n\u003cfigure class=\"dg\" data-rows=\"2\" data-edges=\"n1&gt;n2;n2&gt;n3;n3&gt;n4\" style=\"--rows:2\">\u003cfigcaption class=\"dg-title\">How Connections, pooling and capacity fits together\u003c/figcaption>\u003cdiv class=\"dg-stage\">\u003csvg viewBox=\"0 0 100 100\" preserveAspectRatio=\"none\" aria-hidden=\"true\" focusable=\"false\">\u003cline x1=\"12.5\" y1=\"25.0\" x2=\"37.5\" y2=\"75.0\"/>\u003cline x1=\"37.5\" y1=\"75.0\" x2=\"62.5\" y2=\"25.0\"/>\u003cline x1=\"62.5\" y1=\"25.0\" x2=\"87.5\" y2=\"75.0\"/>\u003c/svg>\u003cbutton type=\"button\" class=\"dg-node k-process\" data-act=\"dg-pick\" data-id=\"n1\" data-kind=\"process\" style=\"left:12.5%;top:25.0%;--w:22.0%\" aria-pressed=\"false\">\u003cspan class=\"dg-name\">Why connections cost\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"dg-kind\">Step\u003c/span>\u003c/button>\u003cbutton type=\"button\" class=\"dg-node k-process\" data-act=\"dg-pick\" data-id=\"n2\" data-kind=\"process\" style=\"left:37.5%;top:75.0%;--w:22.0%\" aria-pressed=\"false\">\u003cspan class=\"dg-name\">Pool maths\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"dg-kind\">Step\u003c/span>\u003c/button>\u003cbutton type=\"button\" class=\"dg-node k-process\" data-act=\"dg-pick\" data-id=\"n3\" data-kind=\"process\" style=\"left:62.5%;top:25.0%;--w:22.0%\" aria-pressed=\"false\">\u003cspan class=\"dg-name\">PgBouncer\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"dg-kind\">Step\u003c/span>\u003c/button>\u003cbutton type=\"button\" class=\"dg-node k-process\" data-act=\"dg-pick\" data-id=\"n4\" data-kind=\"process\" style=\"left:87.5%;top:75.0%;--w:22.0%\" aria-pressed=\"false\">\u003cspan class=\"dg-name\">Memory settings\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"dg-kind\">Step\u003c/span>\u003c/button>\u003c/div>\u003cp class=\"sr\">Flow in order: 1, Why connections cost to Pool maths; 2, Pool maths to PgBouncer; 3, PgBouncer to Memory settings.\u003c/p>\u003cul class=\"dg-legend\" aria-label=\"Legend\">\u003cli>\u003ci class=\"dg-sw k-process\" aria-hidden=\"true\">\u003c/i>Step\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003ci class=\"dg-arrow\" aria-hidden=\"true\">\u003c/i>Numbered arrows show the order of the flow\u003c/li>\u003c/ul>\u003cp class=\"dg-hint small\">Select a component to see what it does and what happens when it fails. \u003cbutton type=\"button\" class=\"btn btn-quiet dg-flow-btn\" data-act=\"dg-flow\" aria-pressed=\"false\">Show the flow\u003c/button>\u003c/p>\u003cdiv class=\"dg-live\" role=\"status\" aria-live=\"polite\">\u003c/div>\u003cdl class=\"dg-list\">\u003cdiv class=\"dg-item\" data-id=\"n1\">\u003cdt>Why connections cost \u003cspan class=\"dg-kt\">Step\u003c/span>\u003c/dt>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">What it does:\u003c/span> PostgreSQL starts a separate process for each client connection.\u003c/dd>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">If it fails or is weak:\u003c/span> Using transaction pooling with an application that depends on session state.\u003c/dd>\u003c/div>\u003cdiv class=\"dg-item\" data-id=\"n2\">\u003cdt>Pool maths \u003cspan class=\"dg-kt\">Step\u003c/span>\u003c/dt>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">What it does:\u003c/span> Each application process has its own pool.\u003c/dd>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">If it fails or is weak:\u003c/span> Not leaving reserved connections for administrators.\u003c/dd>\u003c/div>\u003cdiv class=\"dg-item\" data-id=\"n3\">\u003cdt>PgBouncer \u003cspan class=\"dg-kt\">Step\u003c/span>\u003c/dt>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">What it does:\u003c/span> A pooler sits between applications and the database.\u003c/dd>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">If it fails or is weak:\u003c/span> Raising max_connections instead of fixing the pool sizes.\u003c/dd>\u003c/div>\u003cdiv class=\"dg-item\" data-id=\"n4\">\u003cdt>Memory settings \u003cspan class=\"dg-kt\">Step\u003c/span>\u003c/dt>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">What it does:\u003c/span> shared_buffers: the shared cache. A common starting point is about a quarter of RAM on a dedicated server.\u003c/dd>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">If it fails or is weak:\u003c/span> Setting work_mem very high globally.\u003c/dd>\u003c/div>\u003c/dl>\u003c/figure>\n\u003ch2 id=\"incident-everyone-reconnected-at-once\">Incident: Everyone reconnected at once\u003c/h2>\n\u003caside class=\"callout callout-incident\">\u003cdiv class=\"callout-title\">Incident\u003c/div>\u003cp>After a deploy the database stopped accepting connections for ten minutes.\u003c/p>\n\u003c/aside>\n\u003cdiv class=\"code console\" data-lang=\"terminal\">\u003cbutton class=\"copy\" type=\"button\" data-copy=\"psql -c &quot;SELECT count(*) FROM pg_stat_activity;&quot;\npsql -c &quot;SELECT application_name, count(*) FROM pg_stat_activity GROUP BY 1 ORDER BY 2 DESC LIMIT 2;&quot;\" aria-label=\"Copy commands\">Copy commands\u003c/button>\u003cpre tabindex=\"0\">\u003ccode class=\"hljs\">\u003cspan class=\"ln out\">FATAL:  sorry, too many clients already\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln cmd\">\u003cspan class=\"ps\" aria-hidden=\"true\">$ \u003c/span>psql -c \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">&quot;SELECT count(*) FROM pg_stat_activity;&quot;\u003c/span>\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln out\"> count\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln out\">-------\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln out\">   200\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln cmd\">\u003cspan class=\"ps\" aria-hidden=\"true\">$ \u003c/span>psql -c \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">&quot;SELECT application_name, count(*) FROM pg_stat_activity GROUP BY 1 ORDER BY 2 DESC LIMIT 2;&quot;\u003c/span>\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln out\"> shop-api     | 170\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln out\"> shop-worker  |  26\u003c/span>\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>\n\u003cp>The API had 17 pods with a pool of 10 connections each, and a rolling deploy started new pods before closing the old ones, so for a while there were twice as many. The pool fills with failed connection attempts, requests retry, and the load made recovery slower. The team added PgBouncer in transaction mode, set the pool per pod to 5, and limited the surge during deploys.\u003c/p>\n\u003csection class=\"wp\" aria-label=\"Worked example with a wrong path\">\u003ch4 class=\"wp-h\">Worked example: a tempting wrong path\u003c/h4>\u003cp class=\"wp-goal\">\u003cstrong>Goal.\u003c/strong> The database is out of connections, so raise max_connections to 5000.\u003c/p>\u003cdiv class=\"wp-step wp-bad\">\u003cp class=\"wp-tag\">The tempting way\u003c/p>\u003cpre tabindex=\"0\">\u003ccode>ALTER SYSTEM SET max_connections = 5000;\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>\u003cdetails class=\"wp-more\">\u003csummary>See what goes wrong\u003c/summary>\u003cpre tabindex=\"0\">\u003ccode>(after restart)\n$ free -m\nMemory used 98%\nlatency up, OOM killer ran\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003cp class=\"wp-why\">\u003cstrong>Why it fails.\u003c/strong> Each connection is a process with its own memory, and many active connections cause contention. Raising the limit hides the problem until the server runs out of memory.\u003c/p>\u003c/details>\u003cdetails class=\"wp-more\">\u003csummary>See the way that works\u003c/summary>\u003cdiv class=\"wp-step wp-good\">\u003cp class=\"wp-tag\">The way that works\u003c/p>\u003cpre tabindex=\"0\">\u003ccode># keep max_connections modest and add a pooler\npool_mode = transaction\ndefault_pool_size = 20\nmax_client_conn = 2000\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003cp>Use a pooler so a few server connections serve many clients, and find the leak or idle in transaction sessions.\u003c/p>\u003c/div>\u003c/details>\u003c/section>\n\u003ch2 id=\"common-mistakes\">Common mistakes\u003c/h2>\n\u003cul>\n\u003cli>Raising max_connections instead of fixing the pool sizes\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Setting work_mem very high globally\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Using transaction pooling with an application that depends on session state\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Not leaving reserved connections for administrators\u003c/li>\n\u003c/ul>\n","toc":[{"id":"what-you-will-learn","text":"What you will learn"},{"id":"why-connections-cost","text":"Why connections cost"},{"id":"pool-maths","text":"Pool maths"},{"id":"pgbouncer","text":"PgBouncer"},{"id":"memory-settings","text":"Memory settings"},{"id":"how-the-parts-fit-together","text":"How the parts fit together"},{"id":"incident-everyone-reconnected-at-once","text":"Incident: Everyone reconnected at once"},{"id":"common-mistakes","text":"Common mistakes"}],"refs":[{"t":"PostgreSQL: Connections and authentication","u":"https://www.postgresql.org/docs/current/runtime-config-connection.html"},{"t":"PostgreSQL: Resource consumption","u":"https://www.postgresql.org/docs/current/runtime-config-resource.html"},{"t":"PgBouncer documentation","u":"https://www.pgbouncer.org/config.html"}],"text":"A database does not slow down smoothly when it is overloaded. It runs fine, then it runs out of connections or memory, and fails quickly. The usual cause is that every application instance opens its own pool of connections. What you will learn Why PostgreSQL connections are expensive Connection pool maths for an application fleet PgBouncer and its pooling modes Memory settings: shared_buffers, work_mem and their traps Why connections cost PostgreSQL starts a separate process for each client connection. Each one uses memory and a slot in shared structures, and having hundreds of active sessions makes the CPUs spend time switching between them. A server with 16 cores often performs best with a few dozen running queries, not several hundred. $ psql -c \"SHOW max_connections;\" max_connections ----------------- 200 $ psql -c \"SELECT state, count(*) FROM pg_stat_activity GROUP BY state;\" state | count ----------------------+------- active | 14 idle | 171 idle in transaction | 9 Most of the 194 sessions are idle. They are still using memory and taking slots. When the limit is reached, new clients get: FATAL: sorry, too many clients already Pool maths Each application process has its own pool. With 40 pods and a pool of 10 connections each, the application can open 400 connections, which is more than the database allows. Add a second deployment, a cron job and a reporting tool and the limit is crossed with no one noticing, until a deploy restarts all pods at once and every pod reconnects together. Work out the total: instances × pool size across every client, and keep it below max_connections minus the reserved slots for administrators ( superuser_reserved_connections , 3 by default). PgBouncer A pooler sits between applications and the database. Applications connect to it by the thousands, and it keeps a small number of real connections. Session pooling : a server connection is assigned for the life of the client connection. Safe, but saves little if clients stay connected. Transaction pooling : a server connection is assigned only during a transaction. This is the most useful mode, and it limits what the application may use: session-level features such as SET , advisory locks, LISTEN and (in older versions) prepared statements may not behave as expected. Statement pooling : one statement at a time. Rarely usable, as it forbids multi-statement transactions. [databases] shop = host=10.0.2.10 port=5432 dbname=shop [pgbouncer] pool_mode = transaction max_client_conn = 2000 default_pool_size = 20 With these settings 2 000 clients can connect and at most 20 server connections per database and user pair are used. Memory settings shared_buffers : the shared cache. A common starting point is about a quarter of RAM on a dedicated server. work_mem : memory for each sort or hash step in each query. It is not per connection but per operation, so a complex query can use several times this value, and 200 connections doing so can add up to many gigabytes. Raising it globally is risky; raise it per role or per session for the reporting job. maintenance_work_mem : used by vacuum and index builds. effective_cache_size : tells the planner how much the OS is likely to cache. It allocates nothing. How the parts fit together The lesson in one picture: each box is a section of this lesson, in the order you meet it. Select a box to see what it does and the mistake that usually goes with it. How Connections, pooling and capacity fits together Flow in order: 1, Why connections cost to Pool maths; 2, Pool maths to PgBouncer; 3, PgBouncer to Memory settings. Step Numbered arrows show the order of the flow Select a component to see what it does and what happens when it fails. Why connections cost Step What it does: PostgreSQL starts a separate process for each client connection. If it fails or is weak: Using transaction pooling with an application that depends on session state. Pool maths Step What it does: Each application process has its own pool. If it fails o"},"sql-ops/replication-lag-vacuum-and-day-two-operations":{"html":"\u003cp>A database that works on launch day needs care for years: copies must keep up, old rows must be cleaned out, backups must restore, and changes must not hurt users.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"what-you-will-learn\">What you will learn\u003c/h2>\n\u003cul>\n\u003cli>Streaming replication and how to measure lag\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Vacuum, autovacuum and table bloat\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Safe schema changes\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Backups, point-in-time recovery and restore tests\u003c/li>\n\u003c/ul>\n\u003ch2 id=\"replication-and-lag\">Replication and lag\u003c/h2>\n\u003cp>A \u003cstrong>primary\u003c/strong> sends its write-ahead log (WAL) to \u003cstrong>standbys\u003c/strong>, which replay it. Lag is the delay before a change on the primary is visible on a standby. Reading from a standby that is behind can show old data, such as a user&#39;s order missing right after it was placed.\u003c/p>\n\u003cdiv class=\"code console\" data-lang=\"terminal\">\u003cpre tabindex=\"0\">\u003ccode class=\"hljs\">\u003cspan class=\"ln out\">primary$ psql -c &quot;SELECT client_addr, state, pg_wal_lsn_diff(sent_lsn, replay_lsn) AS bytes_behind, replay_lag FROM pg_stat_replication;&quot;\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln out\"> client_addr | state     | bytes_behind | replay_lag\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln out\">-------------+-----------+--------------+-----------------\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln out\"> 10.0.2.21   | streaming |   1572864000 | 00:07:41.28\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln out\">standby$ psql -c &quot;SELECT now() - pg_last_xact_replay_timestamp() AS delay;&quot;\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln out\"> delay\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln out\">-----------------\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln out\"> 00:07:44.1\u003c/span>\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>\n\u003cp>Common causes of lag: a heavy write burst, a slow standby disk, a long query on the standby that conflicts with replay (the setting \u003ccode>max_standby_streaming_delay\u003c/code> decides who gives way), or the network. A \u003cstrong>replication slot\u003c/strong> keeps WAL for a standby that is offline, and if the standby never returns the primary&#39;s disk fills with WAL. Monitor slots and drop abandoned ones.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"vacuum-and-bloat\">Vacuum and bloat\u003c/h2>\n\u003cp>When a row is updated or deleted, the old version stays until \u003cstrong>vacuum\u003c/strong> removes it. \u003cstrong>Autovacuum\u003c/strong> runs vacuum automatically when about 20 percent of a table (the \u003ccode>autovacuum_vacuum_scale_factor\u003c/code>) plus a small base has changed. On a table with 500 million rows that means 100 million dead rows before it starts, which is far too late. Lower the factor for big, busy tables:\u003c/p>\n\u003cdiv class=\"code\" data-lang=\"text\">\u003cbutton class=\"copy\" type=\"button\" data-copy=\"ALTER TABLE events SET (autovacuum_vacuum_scale_factor = 0.02);\" aria-label=\"Copy code\">Copy\u003c/button>\u003cpre tabindex=\"0\">\u003ccode class=\"hljs\">ALTER TABLE events SET (autovacuum_vacuum_scale_factor = 0.02);\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>\n\u003cdiv class=\"code console\" data-lang=\"terminal\">\u003cbutton class=\"copy\" type=\"button\" data-copy=\"psql -c &quot;SELECT relname, n_live_tup, n_dead_tup, last_autovacuum FROM pg_stat_user_tables ORDER BY n_dead_tup DESC LIMIT 2;&quot;\" aria-label=\"Copy commands\">Copy commands\u003c/button>\u003cpre tabindex=\"0\">\u003ccode class=\"hljs\">\u003cspan class=\"ln cmd\">\u003cspan class=\"ps\" aria-hidden=\"true\">$ \u003c/span>psql -c \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">&quot;SELECT relname, n_live_tup, n_dead_tup, last_autovacuum FROM pg_stat_user_tables ORDER BY n_dead_tup DESC LIMIT 2;&quot;\u003c/span>\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln out\"> relname | n_live_tup | n_dead_tup | last_autovacuum\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln out\">---------+------------+------------+---------------------\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln out\"> events  |  412000000 |   96000000 | 2026-09-30 03:12:10\u003c/span>\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>\n\u003cp>Vacuum cannot remove a row version that some open transaction might still see, so a long transaction (see the locks lesson) keeps dead rows alive. \u003cstrong>Bloat\u003c/strong> is the wasted space left behind. \u003ccode>VACUUM\u003c/code> marks it reusable; it does not shrink the file. To shrink a table, use \u003ccode>pg_repack\u003c/code> or \u003ccode>VACUUM FULL\u003c/code> (which locks the table).\u003c/p>\n\u003cp>A related risk is \u003cstrong>transaction ID wraparound\u003c/strong>: transaction numbers are finite, and vacuum must freeze old rows. If autovacuum cannot keep up, PostgreSQL eventually refuses writes to protect data. Monitor \u003ccode>age(datfrozenxid)\u003c/code>.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"safe-schema-changes\">Safe schema changes\u003c/h2>\n\u003cul>\n\u003cli>Add columns as nullable with no default or with a constant default (cheap on current versions), then backfill in batches.\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Create indexes with \u003ccode>CONCURRENTLY\u003c/code>.\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Add constraints as \u003ccode>NOT VALID\u003c/code> and validate later, so the lock is short.\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Set \u003ccode>lock_timeout\u003c/code> for the migration, and run it when traffic is low.\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Keep changes backward compatible, so old and new application versions both work during a deploy.\u003c/li>\n\u003c/ul>\n\u003ch2 id=\"backups\">Backups\u003c/h2>\n\u003cul>\n\u003cli>\u003cstrong>Logical\u003c/strong> backups (\u003ccode>pg_dump\u003c/code>) are portable and slow to restore on big databases.\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>\u003cstrong>Physical\u003c/strong> backups with WAL archiving (tools such as pgBackRest) allow \u003cstrong>point-in-time recovery\u003c/strong>: restore to the minute before the bad change.\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Replication is not a backup: a mistaken \u003ccode>DELETE\u003c/code> replicates to every standby in seconds.\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Decide RPO and RTO, then restore to a scratch server regularly and run a query that checks the data.\u003c/li>\n\u003c/ul>\n\u003ch2 id=\"how-the-parts-fit-together\">How the parts fit together\u003c/h2>\n\u003cp>The lesson in one picture: each box is a section of this lesson, in the order you meet it. Select a box to see what it does and the mistake that usually goes with it.\u003c/p>\n\u003cfigure class=\"dg\" data-rows=\"2\" data-edges=\"n1&gt;n2;n2&gt;n3;n3&gt;n4\" style=\"--rows:2\">\u003cfigcaption class=\"dg-title\">How Replication lag, vacuum and day-two operations fits together\u003c/figcaption>\u003cdiv class=\"dg-stage\">\u003csvg viewBox=\"0 0 100 100\" preserveAspectRatio=\"none\" aria-hidden=\"true\" focusable=\"false\">\u003cline x1=\"12.5\" y1=\"25.0\" x2=\"37.5\" y2=\"75.0\"/>\u003cline x1=\"37.5\" y1=\"75.0\" x2=\"62.5\" y2=\"25.0\"/>\u003cline x1=\"62.5\" y1=\"25.0\" x2=\"87.5\" y2=\"75.0\"/>\u003c/svg>\u003cbutton type=\"button\" class=\"dg-node k-process\" data-act=\"dg-pick\" data-id=\"n1\" data-kind=\"process\" style=\"left:12.5%;top:25.0%;--w:22.0%\" aria-pressed=\"false\">\u003cspan class=\"dg-name\">Replication and lag\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"dg-kind\">Step\u003c/span>\u003c/button>\u003cbutton type=\"button\" class=\"dg-node k-process\" data-act=\"dg-pick\" data-id=\"n2\" data-kind=\"process\" style=\"left:37.5%;top:75.0%;--w:22.0%\" aria-pressed=\"false\">\u003cspan class=\"dg-name\">Vacuum and bloat\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"dg-kind\">Step\u003c/span>\u003c/button>\u003cbutton type=\"button\" class=\"dg-node k-process\" data-act=\"dg-pick\" data-id=\"n3\" data-kind=\"process\" style=\"left:62.5%;top:25.0%;--w:22.0%\" aria-pressed=\"false\">\u003cspan class=\"dg-name\">Safe schema changes\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"dg-kind\">Step\u003c/span>\u003c/button>\u003cbutton type=\"button\" class=\"dg-node k-store\" data-act=\"dg-pick\" data-id=\"n4\" data-kind=\"store\" style=\"left:87.5%;top:75.0%;--w:22.0%\" aria-pressed=\"false\">\u003cspan class=\"dg-name\">Backups\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"dg-kind\">Data store\u003c/span>\u003c/button>\u003c/div>\u003cp class=\"sr\">Flow in order: 1, Replication and lag to Vacuum and bloat; 2, Vacuum and bloat to Safe schema changes; 3, Safe schema changes to Backups.\u003c/p>\u003cul class=\"dg-legend\" aria-label=\"Legend\">\u003cli>\u003ci class=\"dg-sw k-process\" aria-hidden=\"true\">\u003c/i>Step\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003ci class=\"dg-sw k-store\" aria-hidden=\"true\">\u003c/i>Data store\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003ci class=\"dg-arrow\" aria-hidden=\"true\">\u003c/i>Numbered arrows show the order of the flow\u003c/li>\u003c/ul>\u003cp class=\"dg-hint small\">Select a component to see what it does and what happens when it fails. \u003cbutton type=\"button\" class=\"btn btn-quiet dg-flow-btn\" data-act=\"dg-flow\" aria-pressed=\"false\">Show the flow\u003c/button>\u003c/p>\u003cdiv class=\"dg-live\" role=\"status\" aria-live=\"polite\">\u003c/div>\u003cdl class=\"dg-list\">\u003cdiv class=\"dg-item\" data-id=\"n1\">\u003cdt>Replication and lag \u003cspan class=\"dg-kt\">Step\u003c/span>\u003c/dt>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">What it does:\u003c/span> A primary sends its write-ahead log (WAL) to standbys, which replay it.\u003c/dd>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">If it fails or is weak:\u003c/span> Reading important data from a lagging standby.\u003c/dd>\u003c/div>\u003cdiv class=\"dg-item\" data-id=\"n2\">\u003cdt>Vacuum and bloat \u003cspan class=\"dg-kt\">Step\u003c/span>\u003c/dt>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">What it does:\u003c/span> When a row is updated or deleted, the old version stays until vacuum removes it.\u003c/dd>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">If it fails or is weak:\u003c/span> Leaving autovacuum defaults on very large tables.\u003c/dd>\u003c/div>\u003cdiv class=\"dg-item\" data-id=\"n3\">\u003cdt>Safe schema changes \u003cspan class=\"dg-kt\">Step\u003c/span>\u003c/dt>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">What it does:\u003c/span> Add columns as nullable with no default or with a constant default (cheap on current versions), then backfill in batches.\u003c/dd>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">If it fails or is weak:\u003c/span> Check the Common mistakes list below this diagram for what usually goes wrong here.\u003c/dd>\u003c/div>\u003cdiv class=\"dg-item\" data-id=\"n4\">\u003cdt>Backups \u003cspan class=\"dg-kt\">Data store\u003c/span>\u003c/dt>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">What it does:\u003c/span> Logical backups (pg_dump) are portable and slow to restore on big databases.\u003c/dd>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">If it fails or is weak:\u003c/span> Believing a replica is a backup.\u003c/dd>\u003c/div>\u003c/dl>\u003c/figure>\n\u003ch2 id=\"incident-the-standby-that-was-always-behind\">Incident: The standby that was always behind\u003c/h2>\n\u003caside class=\"callout callout-incident\">\u003cdiv class=\"callout-title\">Incident\u003c/div>\u003cp>Customers saw their new orders missing for several minutes, and then the primary ran out of disk.\u003c/p>\n\u003c/aside>\n\u003cdiv class=\"code console\" data-lang=\"terminal\">\u003cpre tabindex=\"0\">\u003ccode class=\"hljs\">\u003cspan class=\"ln out\">primary$ psql -c &quot;SELECT slot_name, active, pg_size_pretty(pg_wal_lsn_diff(pg_current_wal_lsn(), restart_lsn)) AS retained FROM pg_replication_slots;&quot;\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln out\"> slot_name    | active | retained\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln out\">--------------+--------+----------\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln out\"> reporting_s1 | f      | 412 GB\u003c/span>\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>\n\u003cp>A reporting replica had been switched off a week earlier but its replication slot remained. The primary kept every WAL file since then, 412 GB, until the disk filled. Another standby was lagging because a long report on it conflicted with replay. Dropping the dead slot with \u003ccode>pg_drop_replication_slot\u003c/code> freed the disk, and reports were moved to a replica with its own conflict settings. Replication needs monitoring just as the primary does.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"common-mistakes\">Common mistakes\u003c/h2>\n\u003cul>\n\u003cli>Reading important data from a lagging standby\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Leaving autovacuum defaults on very large tables\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Believing a replica is a backup\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Never restoring a backup to see if it works\u003c/li>\n\u003c/ul>\n","toc":[{"id":"what-you-will-learn","text":"What you will learn"},{"id":"replication-and-lag","text":"Replication and lag"},{"id":"vacuum-and-bloat","text":"Vacuum and bloat"},{"id":"safe-schema-changes","text":"Safe schema changes"},{"id":"backups","text":"Backups"},{"id":"how-the-parts-fit-together","text":"How the parts fit together"},{"id":"incident-the-standby-that-was-always-behind","text":"Incident: The standby that was always behind"},{"id":"common-mistakes","text":"Common mistakes"}],"refs":[{"t":"PostgreSQL: High availability and replication","u":"https://www.postgresql.org/docs/current/high-availability.html"},{"t":"PostgreSQL: Routine vacuuming","u":"https://www.postgresql.org/docs/current/routine-vacuuming.html"},{"t":"pgBackRest documentation","u":"https://pgbackrest.org/user-guide.html"}],"text":"A database that works on launch day needs care for years: copies must keep up, old rows must be cleaned out, backups must restore, and changes must not hurt users. What you will learn Streaming replication and how to measure lag Vacuum, autovacuum and table bloat Safe schema changes Backups, point-in-time recovery and restore tests Replication and lag A primary sends its write-ahead log (WAL) to standbys , which replay it. Lag is the delay before a change on the primary is visible on a standby. Reading from a standby that is behind can show old data, such as a user&#39;s order missing right after it was placed. primary$ psql -c \"SELECT client_addr, state, pg_wal_lsn_diff(sent_lsn, replay_lsn) AS bytes_behind, replay_lag FROM pg_stat_replication;\" client_addr | state | bytes_behind | replay_lag -------------+-----------+--------------+----------------- 10.0.2.21 | streaming | 1572864000 | 00:07:41.28 standby$ psql -c \"SELECT now() - pg_last_xact_replay_timestamp() AS delay;\" delay ----------------- 00:07:44.1 Common causes of lag: a heavy write burst, a slow standby disk, a long query on the standby that conflicts with replay (the setting max_standby_streaming_delay decides who gives way), or the network. A replication slot keeps WAL for a standby that is offline, and if the standby never returns the primary&#39;s disk fills with WAL. Monitor slots and drop abandoned ones. Vacuum and bloat When a row is updated or deleted, the old version stays until vacuum removes it. Autovacuum runs vacuum automatically when about 20 percent of a table (the autovacuum_vacuum_scale_factor ) plus a small base has changed. On a table with 500 million rows that means 100 million dead rows before it starts, which is far too late. Lower the factor for big, busy tables: ALTER TABLE events SET (autovacuum_vacuum_scale_factor = 0.02); $ psql -c \"SELECT relname, n_live_tup, n_dead_tup, last_autovacuum FROM pg_stat_user_tables ORDER BY n_dead_tup DESC LIMIT 2;\" relname | n_live_tup | n_dead_tup | last_autovacuum ---------+------------+------------+--------------------- events | 412000000 | 96000000 | 2026-09-30 03:12:10 Vacuum cannot remove a row version that some open transaction might still see, so a long transaction (see the locks lesson) keeps dead rows alive. Bloat is the wasted space left behind. VACUUM marks it reusable; it does not shrink the file. To shrink a table, use pg_repack or VACUUM FULL (which locks the table). A related risk is transaction ID wraparound : transaction numbers are finite, and vacuum must freeze old rows. If autovacuum cannot keep up, PostgreSQL eventually refuses writes to protect data. Monitor age(datfrozenxid) . Safe schema changes Add columns as nullable with no default or with a constant default (cheap on current versions), then backfill in batches. Create indexes with CONCURRENTLY . Add constraints as NOT VALID and validate later, so the lock is short. Set lock_timeout for the migration, and run it when traffic is low. Keep changes backward compatible, so old and new application versions both work during a deploy. Backups Logical backups ( pg_dump ) are portable and slow to restore on big databases. Physical backups with WAL archiving (tools such as pgBackRest) allow point-in-time recovery : restore to the minute before the bad change. Replication is not a backup: a mistaken DELETE replicates to every standby in seconds. Decide RPO and RTO, then restore to a scratch server regularly and run a query that checks the data. How the parts fit together The lesson in one picture: each box is a section of this lesson, in the order you meet it. Select a box to see what it does and the mistake that usually goes with it. How Replication lag, vacuum and day-two operations fits together Flow in order: 1, Replication and lag to Vacuum and bloat; 2, Vacuum and bloat to Safe schema changes; 3, Safe schema changes to Backups. Step Data store Numbered arrows show the order of the flow Select a component to see what it does and what h"},"sql-ops/pg-stat-statements-and-query-tuning":{"html":"\u003cp>Slow-query logs show single bad events, but load comes from patterns. The pg_stat_statements extension groups queries by shape and tells you where the database spends its time, so you can spend yours on the right query.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"what-you-will-learn\">What you will learn\u003c/h2>\n\u003cul>\n\u003cli>Enabling and reading pg_stat_statements\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Ranking by total time, mean time and calls\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Moving from a query to its plan and fix\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Measuring the effect of a change\u003c/li>\n\u003c/ul>\n\u003ch2 id=\"enable-and-read\">Enable and read\u003c/h2>\n\u003cdiv class=\"code\" data-lang=\"sql\">\u003cbutton class=\"copy\" type=\"button\" data-copy=\"-- postgresql.conf: shared_preload_libraries = 'pg_stat_statements'\nCREATE EXTENSION IF NOT EXISTS pg_stat_statements;\n\nSELECT calls,\n       round(total_exec_time::numeric, 0) AS total_ms,\n       round(mean_exec_time::numeric, 2)  AS mean_ms,\n       rows,\n       left(query, 80) AS query\nFROM pg_stat_statements\nORDER BY total_exec_time DESC\nLIMIT 10;\" aria-label=\"Copy code\">Copy\u003c/button>\u003cpre tabindex=\"0\">\u003ccode class=\"hljs\">\u003cspan class=\"hljs-comment\">-- postgresql.conf: shared_preload_libraries = &#x27;pg_stat_statements&#x27;\u003c/span>\n\u003cspan class=\"hljs-keyword\">CREATE\u003c/span> EXTENSION IF \u003cspan class=\"hljs-keyword\">NOT\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-keyword\">EXISTS\u003c/span> pg_stat_statements;\n\n\u003cspan class=\"hljs-keyword\">SELECT\u003c/span> calls,\n       round(total_exec_time::\u003cspan class=\"hljs-type\">numeric\u003c/span>, \u003cspan class=\"hljs-number\">0\u003c/span>) \u003cspan class=\"hljs-keyword\">AS\u003c/span> total_ms,\n       round(mean_exec_time::\u003cspan class=\"hljs-type\">numeric\u003c/span>, \u003cspan class=\"hljs-number\">2\u003c/span>)  \u003cspan class=\"hljs-keyword\">AS\u003c/span> mean_ms,\n       \u003cspan class=\"hljs-keyword\">rows\u003c/span>,\n       \u003cspan class=\"hljs-keyword\">left\u003c/span>(query, \u003cspan class=\"hljs-number\">80\u003c/span>) \u003cspan class=\"hljs-keyword\">AS\u003c/span> query\n\u003cspan class=\"hljs-keyword\">FROM\u003c/span> pg_stat_statements\n\u003cspan class=\"hljs-keyword\">ORDER\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-keyword\">BY\u003c/span> total_exec_time \u003cspan class=\"hljs-keyword\">DESC\u003c/span>\nLIMIT \u003cspan class=\"hljs-number\">10\u003c/span>;\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>\n\u003cp>Queries are \u003cstrong>normalized\u003c/strong>, so \u003ccode>WHERE id = 7\u003c/code> and \u003ccode>WHERE id = 9\u003c/code> count as one entry with a placeholder. Sort three ways: by total time for overall load, by mean time for slow single calls and by calls for chatty clients.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"from-query-to-fix\">From query to fix\u003c/h2>\n\u003cdiv class=\"code\" data-lang=\"sql\">\u003cbutton class=\"copy\" type=\"button\" data-copy=\"EXPLAIN (ANALYZE, BUFFERS)\nSELECT * FROM orders WHERE customer_id = 42 AND status = 'open';\n\nCREATE INDEX CONCURRENTLY orders_customer_status_idx\n  ON orders (customer_id, status);\" aria-label=\"Copy code\">Copy\u003c/button>\u003cpre tabindex=\"0\">\u003ccode class=\"hljs\">EXPLAIN (ANALYZE, BUFFERS)\n\u003cspan class=\"hljs-keyword\">SELECT\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-operator\">*\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-keyword\">FROM\u003c/span> orders \u003cspan class=\"hljs-keyword\">WHERE\u003c/span> customer_id \u003cspan class=\"hljs-operator\">=\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-number\">42\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-keyword\">AND\u003c/span> status \u003cspan class=\"hljs-operator\">=\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">&#x27;open&#x27;\u003c/span>;\n\n\u003cspan class=\"hljs-keyword\">CREATE\u003c/span> INDEX CONCURRENTLY orders_customer_status_idx\n  \u003cspan class=\"hljs-keyword\">ON\u003c/span> orders (customer_id, status);\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>\n\u003cp>Look for sequential scans on large tables, row estimates far from actual rows and many shared buffers read. Typical fixes are a suitable index, selecting only needed columns, removing an unneeded join, batching many small queries into one or caching the result in the application.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"measure\">Measure\u003c/h2>\n\u003cdiv class=\"code\" data-lang=\"sql\">\u003cbutton class=\"copy\" type=\"button\" data-copy=\"SELECT pg_stat_statements_reset();\n-- run normal traffic for an hour, then query again\" aria-label=\"Copy code\">Copy\u003c/button>\u003cpre tabindex=\"0\">\u003ccode class=\"hljs\">\u003cspan class=\"hljs-keyword\">SELECT\u003c/span> pg_stat_statements_reset();\n\u003cspan class=\"hljs-comment\">-- run normal traffic for an hour, then query again\u003c/span>\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>\n\u003cp>Reset the counters before a change and compare after a representative period. Record the before and after total time so the improvement is evidence rather than a feeling.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"how-the-parts-fit-together\">How the parts fit together\u003c/h2>\n\u003cp>The lesson in one picture: each box is a section of this lesson, in the order you meet it. Select a box to see what it does and the mistake that usually goes with it.\u003c/p>\n\u003cfigure class=\"dg\" data-rows=\"2\" data-edges=\"n1&gt;n2;n2&gt;n3\" style=\"--rows:2\">\u003cfigcaption class=\"dg-title\">How Finding the worst queries with pg_stat_statements fits together\u003c/figcaption>\u003cdiv class=\"dg-stage\">\u003csvg viewBox=\"0 0 100 100\" preserveAspectRatio=\"none\" aria-hidden=\"true\" focusable=\"false\">\u003cline x1=\"16.7\" y1=\"25.0\" x2=\"50.0\" y2=\"75.0\"/>\u003cline x1=\"50.0\" y1=\"75.0\" x2=\"83.3\" y2=\"25.0\"/>\u003c/svg>\u003cbutton type=\"button\" class=\"dg-node k-process\" data-act=\"dg-pick\" data-id=\"n1\" data-kind=\"process\" style=\"left:16.7%;top:25.0%;--w:29.3%\" aria-pressed=\"false\">\u003cspan class=\"dg-name\">Enable and read\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"dg-kind\">Step\u003c/span>\u003c/button>\u003cbutton type=\"button\" class=\"dg-node k-process\" data-act=\"dg-pick\" data-id=\"n2\" data-kind=\"process\" style=\"left:50.0%;top:75.0%;--w:29.3%\" aria-pressed=\"false\">\u003cspan class=\"dg-name\">From query to fix\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"dg-kind\">Step\u003c/span>\u003c/button>\u003cbutton type=\"button\" class=\"dg-node k-process\" data-act=\"dg-pick\" data-id=\"n3\" data-kind=\"process\" style=\"left:83.3%;top:25.0%;--w:29.3%\" aria-pressed=\"false\">\u003cspan class=\"dg-name\">Measure\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"dg-kind\">Step\u003c/span>\u003c/button>\u003c/div>\u003cp class=\"sr\">Flow in order: 1, Enable and read to From query to fix; 2, From query to fix to Measure.\u003c/p>\u003cul class=\"dg-legend\" aria-label=\"Legend\">\u003cli>\u003ci class=\"dg-sw k-process\" aria-hidden=\"true\">\u003c/i>Step\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003ci class=\"dg-arrow\" aria-hidden=\"true\">\u003c/i>Numbered arrows show the order of the flow\u003c/li>\u003c/ul>\u003cp class=\"dg-hint small\">Select a component to see what it does and what happens when it fails. \u003cbutton type=\"button\" class=\"btn btn-quiet dg-flow-btn\" data-act=\"dg-flow\" aria-pressed=\"false\">Show the flow\u003c/button>\u003c/p>\u003cdiv class=\"dg-live\" role=\"status\" aria-live=\"polite\">\u003c/div>\u003cdl class=\"dg-list\">\u003cdiv class=\"dg-item\" data-id=\"n1\">\u003cdt>Enable and read \u003cspan class=\"dg-kt\">Step\u003c/span>\u003c/dt>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">What it does:\u003c/span> Queries are normalized, so WHERE id = 7 and WHERE id = 9 count as one entry with a placeholder.\u003c/dd>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">If it fails or is weak:\u003c/span> Tuning only the slowest single query.\u003c/dd>\u003c/div>\u003cdiv class=\"dg-item\" data-id=\"n2\">\u003cdt>From query to fix \u003cspan class=\"dg-kt\">Step\u003c/span>\u003c/dt>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">What it does:\u003c/span> Look for sequential scans on large tables, row estimates far from actual rows and many shared buffers read.\u003c/dd>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">If it fails or is weak:\u003c/span> Reading plans without ANALYZE and BUFFERS.\u003c/dd>\u003c/div>\u003cdiv class=\"dg-item\" data-id=\"n3\">\u003cdt>Measure \u003cspan class=\"dg-kt\">Step\u003c/span>\u003c/dt>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">What it does:\u003c/span> Reset the counters before a change and compare after a representative period.\u003c/dd>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">If it fails or is weak:\u003c/span> Not resetting statistics before a test.\u003c/dd>\u003c/div>\u003c/dl>\u003c/figure>\n\u003ch2 id=\"incident-the-fast-query-that-used-all-the-cpu\">Incident: The fast query that used all the CPU\u003c/h2>\n\u003caside class=\"callout callout-incident\">\u003cdiv class=\"callout-title\">Incident\u003c/div>\u003cp>A cheap query called constantly dominated database load.\u003c/p>\n\u003c/aside>\n\u003cdiv class=\"code console\" data-lang=\"terminal\">\u003cpre tabindex=\"0\">\u003ccode class=\"hljs\">\u003cspan class=\"ln out\">calls: 18400000  mean_ms: 4.1  total_ms: 75440000\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln out\">query: SELECT * FROM sessions WHERE token = $1\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln out\">Seq Scan on sessions (rows=1 of 2,100,000)\u003c/span>\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>\n\u003cp>The team had chased long reports. Ranking by total time exposed a session lookup with no index on token. One index removed the scan, brought the mean time under 0.1 ms and cut CPU by half.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"common-mistakes\">Common mistakes\u003c/h2>\n\u003cul>\n\u003cli>Tuning only the slowest single query\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Not resetting statistics before a test\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Adding indexes without checking write cost\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Reading plans without ANALYZE and BUFFERS\u003c/li>\n\u003c/ul>\n","toc":[{"id":"what-you-will-learn","text":"What you will learn"},{"id":"enable-and-read","text":"Enable and read"},{"id":"from-query-to-fix","text":"From query to fix"},{"id":"measure","text":"Measure"},{"id":"how-the-parts-fit-together","text":"How the parts fit together"},{"id":"incident-the-fast-query-that-used-all-the-cpu","text":"Incident: The fast query that used all the CPU"},{"id":"common-mistakes","text":"Common mistakes"}],"refs":[{"t":"PostgreSQL pg_stat_statements","u":"https://www.postgresql.org/docs/current/pgstatstatements.html"},{"t":"PostgreSQL using EXPLAIN","u":"https://www.postgresql.org/docs/current/using-explain.html"}],"text":"Slow-query logs show single bad events, but load comes from patterns. The pg_stat_statements extension groups queries by shape and tells you where the database spends its time, so you can spend yours on the right query. What you will learn Enabling and reading pg_stat_statements Ranking by total time, mean time and calls Moving from a query to its plan and fix Measuring the effect of a change Enable and read -- postgresql.conf: shared_preload_libraries = &#x27;pg_stat_statements&#x27; CREATE EXTENSION IF NOT EXISTS pg_stat_statements; SELECT calls, round(total_exec_time:: numeric , 0 ) AS total_ms, round(mean_exec_time:: numeric , 2 ) AS mean_ms, rows , left (query, 80 ) AS query FROM pg_stat_statements ORDER BY total_exec_time DESC LIMIT 10 ; Queries are normalized , so WHERE id = 7 and WHERE id = 9 count as one entry with a placeholder. Sort three ways: by total time for overall load, by mean time for slow single calls and by calls for chatty clients. From query to fix EXPLAIN (ANALYZE, BUFFERS) SELECT * FROM orders WHERE customer_id = 42 AND status = &#x27;open&#x27; ; CREATE INDEX CONCURRENTLY orders_customer_status_idx ON orders (customer_id, status); Look for sequential scans on large tables, row estimates far from actual rows and many shared buffers read. Typical fixes are a suitable index, selecting only needed columns, removing an unneeded join, batching many small queries into one or caching the result in the application. Measure SELECT pg_stat_statements_reset(); -- run normal traffic for an hour, then query again Reset the counters before a change and compare after a representative period. Record the before and after total time so the improvement is evidence rather than a feeling. How the parts fit together The lesson in one picture: each box is a section of this lesson, in the order you meet it. Select a box to see what it does and the mistake that usually goes with it. How Finding the worst queries with pg_stat_statements fits together Flow in order: 1, Enable and read to From query to fix; 2, From query to fix to Measure. Step Numbered arrows show the order of the flow Select a component to see what it does and what happens when it fails. Enable and read Step What it does: Queries are normalized, so WHERE id = 7 and WHERE id = 9 count as one entry with a placeholder. If it fails or is weak: Tuning only the slowest single query. From query to fix Step What it does: Look for sequential scans on large tables, row estimates far from actual rows and many shared buffers read. If it fails or is weak: Reading plans without ANALYZE and BUFFERS. Measure Step What it does: Reset the counters before a change and compare after a representative period. If it fails or is weak: Not resetting statistics before a test. Incident: The fast query that used all the CPU Incident A cheap query called constantly dominated database load. calls: 18400000 mean_ms: 4.1 total_ms: 75440000 query: SELECT * FROM sessions WHERE token = $1 Seq Scan on sessions (rows=1 of 2,100,000) The team had chased long reports. Ranking by total time exposed a session lookup with no index on token. One index removed the scan, brought the mean time under 0.1 ms and cut CPU by half. Common mistakes Tuning only the slowest single query Not resetting statistics before a test Adding indexes without checking write cost Reading plans without ANALYZE and BUFFERS"},"sql-ops/partitioning-and-data-retention":{"html":"\u003cp>A table with billions of rows can still be fast to query, but it becomes hard to maintain: deletes are slow, vacuum takes forever and indexes grow huge. Partitioning keeps data in manageable pieces and makes retention a metadata operation.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"what-you-will-learn\">What you will learn\u003c/h2>\n\u003cul>\n\u003cli>Range, list and hash partitioning and when to use each\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Creating partitions and automating them\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Dropping and detaching old data safely\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Making queries use partition pruning\u003c/li>\n\u003c/ul>\n\u003ch2 id=\"declarative-partitioning\">Declarative partitioning\u003c/h2>\n\u003cdiv class=\"code\" data-lang=\"sql\">\u003cbutton class=\"copy\" type=\"button\" data-copy=\"CREATE TABLE events (\n    id bigint GENERATED ALWAYS AS IDENTITY,\n    created_at timestamptz NOT NULL,\n    kind text NOT NULL,\n    payload jsonb\n) PARTITION BY RANGE (created_at);\n\nCREATE TABLE events_2026_11 PARTITION OF events\n    FOR VALUES FROM ('2026-11-01') TO ('2026-12-01');\nCREATE INDEX ON events_2026_11 (created_at, kind);\" aria-label=\"Copy code\">Copy\u003c/button>\u003cpre tabindex=\"0\">\u003ccode class=\"hljs\">\u003cspan class=\"hljs-keyword\">CREATE TABLE\u003c/span> events (\n    id \u003cspan class=\"hljs-type\">bigint\u003c/span> GENERATED ALWAYS \u003cspan class=\"hljs-keyword\">AS\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-keyword\">IDENTITY\u003c/span>,\n    created_at timestamptz \u003cspan class=\"hljs-keyword\">NOT NULL\u003c/span>,\n    kind text \u003cspan class=\"hljs-keyword\">NOT NULL\u003c/span>,\n    payload jsonb\n) \u003cspan class=\"hljs-keyword\">PARTITION\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-keyword\">BY\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-keyword\">RANGE\u003c/span> (created_at);\n\n\u003cspan class=\"hljs-keyword\">CREATE TABLE\u003c/span> events_2026_11 \u003cspan class=\"hljs-keyword\">PARTITION\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-keyword\">OF\u003c/span> events\n    \u003cspan class=\"hljs-keyword\">FOR\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-keyword\">VALUES\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-keyword\">FROM\u003c/span> (\u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">&#x27;2026-11-01&#x27;\u003c/span>) \u003cspan class=\"hljs-keyword\">TO\u003c/span> (\u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">&#x27;2026-12-01&#x27;\u003c/span>);\n\u003cspan class=\"hljs-keyword\">CREATE\u003c/span> INDEX \u003cspan class=\"hljs-keyword\">ON\u003c/span> events_2026_11 (created_at, kind);\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>\n\u003cp>\u003cstrong>Range\u003c/strong> partitions by time suit logs and events. \u003cstrong>List\u003c/strong> partitions split by a category such as region. \u003cstrong>Hash\u003c/strong> spreads rows evenly across a fixed number of partitions. Primary keys and unique constraints must include the partition key.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"automate-and-retire\">Automate and retire\u003c/h2>\n\u003cp>Create next month&#39;s partition before it is needed, with a scheduled job or an extension, and alert if it is missing, because inserts outside every range fail. Retire old data by detaching, archiving and dropping:\u003c/p>\n\u003cdiv class=\"code\" data-lang=\"sql\">\u003cbutton class=\"copy\" type=\"button\" data-copy=\"ALTER TABLE events DETACH PARTITION events_2025_11 CONCURRENTLY;\n-- archive with pg_dump if required, then:\nDROP TABLE events_2025_11;\" aria-label=\"Copy code\">Copy\u003c/button>\u003cpre tabindex=\"0\">\u003ccode class=\"hljs\">\u003cspan class=\"hljs-keyword\">ALTER TABLE\u003c/span> events DETACH \u003cspan class=\"hljs-keyword\">PARTITION\u003c/span> events_2025_11 CONCURRENTLY;\n\u003cspan class=\"hljs-comment\">-- archive with pg_dump if required, then:\u003c/span>\n\u003cspan class=\"hljs-keyword\">DROP\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-keyword\">TABLE\u003c/span> events_2025_11;\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>\n\u003cp>Dropping a partition is fast and does not create dead rows, unlike deleting millions of rows.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"pruning\">Pruning\u003c/h2>\n\u003cdiv class=\"code\" data-lang=\"sql\">\u003cbutton class=\"copy\" type=\"button\" data-copy=\"EXPLAIN SELECT count(*) FROM events\nWHERE created_at &gt;= '2026-11-01' AND created_at &lt; '2026-11-08';\" aria-label=\"Copy code\">Copy\u003c/button>\u003cpre tabindex=\"0\">\u003ccode class=\"hljs\">EXPLAIN \u003cspan class=\"hljs-keyword\">SELECT\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-built_in\">count\u003c/span>(\u003cspan class=\"hljs-operator\">*\u003c/span>) \u003cspan class=\"hljs-keyword\">FROM\u003c/span> events\n\u003cspan class=\"hljs-keyword\">WHERE\u003c/span> created_at \u003cspan class=\"hljs-operator\">&gt;=\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">&#x27;2026-11-01&#x27;\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-keyword\">AND\u003c/span> created_at \u003cspan class=\"hljs-operator\">&lt;\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">&#x27;2026-11-08&#x27;\u003c/span>;\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>\n\u003cp>If the plan shows only the relevant partitions, \u003cstrong>pruning\u003c/strong> works. Queries that omit the partition key scan all partitions. Keep the number of partitions reasonable, because thousands of tiny partitions slow planning.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"how-the-parts-fit-together\">How the parts fit together\u003c/h2>\n\u003cp>The lesson in one picture: each box is a section of this lesson, in the order you meet it. Select a box to see what it does and the mistake that usually goes with it.\u003c/p>\n\u003cfigure class=\"dg\" data-rows=\"2\" data-edges=\"n1&gt;n2;n2&gt;n3\" style=\"--rows:2\">\u003cfigcaption class=\"dg-title\">How Partitioning and data retention fits together\u003c/figcaption>\u003cdiv class=\"dg-stage\">\u003csvg viewBox=\"0 0 100 100\" preserveAspectRatio=\"none\" aria-hidden=\"true\" focusable=\"false\">\u003cline x1=\"16.7\" y1=\"25.0\" x2=\"50.0\" y2=\"75.0\"/>\u003cline x1=\"50.0\" y1=\"75.0\" x2=\"83.3\" y2=\"25.0\"/>\u003c/svg>\u003cbutton type=\"button\" class=\"dg-node k-process\" data-act=\"dg-pick\" data-id=\"n1\" data-kind=\"process\" style=\"left:16.7%;top:25.0%;--w:29.3%\" aria-pressed=\"false\">\u003cspan class=\"dg-name\">Declarative partitioning\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"dg-kind\">Step\u003c/span>\u003c/button>\u003cbutton type=\"button\" class=\"dg-node k-process\" data-act=\"dg-pick\" data-id=\"n2\" data-kind=\"process\" style=\"left:50.0%;top:75.0%;--w:29.3%\" aria-pressed=\"false\">\u003cspan class=\"dg-name\">Automate and retire\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"dg-kind\">Step\u003c/span>\u003c/button>\u003cbutton type=\"button\" class=\"dg-node k-process\" data-act=\"dg-pick\" data-id=\"n3\" data-kind=\"process\" style=\"left:83.3%;top:25.0%;--w:29.3%\" aria-pressed=\"false\">\u003cspan class=\"dg-name\">Pruning\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"dg-kind\">Step\u003c/span>\u003c/button>\u003c/div>\u003cp class=\"sr\">Flow in order: 1, Declarative partitioning to Automate and retire; 2, Automate and retire to Pruning.\u003c/p>\u003cul class=\"dg-legend\" aria-label=\"Legend\">\u003cli>\u003ci class=\"dg-sw k-process\" aria-hidden=\"true\">\u003c/i>Step\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003ci class=\"dg-arrow\" aria-hidden=\"true\">\u003c/i>Numbered arrows show the order of the flow\u003c/li>\u003c/ul>\u003cp class=\"dg-hint small\">Select a component to see what it does and what happens when it fails. \u003cbutton type=\"button\" class=\"btn btn-quiet dg-flow-btn\" data-act=\"dg-flow\" aria-pressed=\"false\">Show the flow\u003c/button>\u003c/p>\u003cdiv class=\"dg-live\" role=\"status\" aria-live=\"polite\">\u003c/div>\u003cdl class=\"dg-list\">\u003cdiv class=\"dg-item\" data-id=\"n1\">\u003cdt>Declarative partitioning \u003cspan class=\"dg-kt\">Step\u003c/span>\u003c/dt>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">What it does:\u003c/span> Range partitions by time suit logs and events.\u003c/dd>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">If it fails or is weak:\u003c/span> Forgetting to create future partitions.\u003c/dd>\u003c/div>\u003cdiv class=\"dg-item\" data-id=\"n2\">\u003cdt>Automate and retire \u003cspan class=\"dg-kt\">Step\u003c/span>\u003c/dt>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">What it does:\u003c/span> Create next month's partition before it is needed, with a scheduled job or an extension, and alert if it is missing, because inserts outside every range fail.\u003c/dd>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">If it fails or is weak:\u003c/span> Deleting rows instead of dropping partitions.\u003c/dd>\u003c/div>\u003cdiv class=\"dg-item\" data-id=\"n3\">\u003cdt>Pruning \u003cspan class=\"dg-kt\">Step\u003c/span>\u003c/dt>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">What it does:\u003c/span> If the plan shows only the relevant partitions, pruning works.\u003c/dd>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">If it fails or is weak:\u003c/span> Queries that do not use the partition key.\u003c/dd>\u003c/div>\u003c/dl>\u003c/figure>\n\u003ch2 id=\"incident-no-partition-for-today\">Incident: No partition for today\u003c/h2>\n\u003caside class=\"callout callout-incident\">\u003cdiv class=\"callout-title\">Incident\u003c/div>\u003cp>Inserts failed at midnight because the next partition did not exist.\u003c/p>\n\u003c/aside>\n\u003cdiv class=\"code console\" data-lang=\"terminal\">\u003cpre tabindex=\"0\">\u003ccode class=\"hljs\">\u003cspan class=\"ln out\">ERROR:  no partition of relation &quot;events&quot; found for row\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln out\">DETAIL:  Partition key of the failing row contains (created_at) = (2026-12-01 00:00:03+00).\u003c/span>\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>\n\u003cp>The job that created monthly partitions had been failing silently for weeks. At the month boundary writes began to fail. The team added a default partition as a safety net, alerts for missing future partitions and a test that checks three months ahead.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"common-mistakes\">Common mistakes\u003c/h2>\n\u003cul>\n\u003cli>Forgetting to create future partitions\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Deleting rows instead of dropping partitions\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Queries that do not use the partition key\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Creating thousands of tiny partitions\u003c/li>\n\u003c/ul>\n","toc":[{"id":"what-you-will-learn","text":"What you will learn"},{"id":"declarative-partitioning","text":"Declarative partitioning"},{"id":"automate-and-retire","text":"Automate and retire"},{"id":"pruning","text":"Pruning"},{"id":"how-the-parts-fit-together","text":"How the parts fit together"},{"id":"incident-no-partition-for-today","text":"Incident: No partition for today"},{"id":"common-mistakes","text":"Common mistakes"}],"refs":[{"t":"PostgreSQL table partitioning","u":"https://www.postgresql.org/docs/current/ddl-partitioning.html"},{"t":"PostgreSQL routine vacuuming","u":"https://www.postgresql.org/docs/current/routine-vacuuming.html"}],"text":"A table with billions of rows can still be fast to query, but it becomes hard to maintain: deletes are slow, vacuum takes forever and indexes grow huge. Partitioning keeps data in manageable pieces and makes retention a metadata operation. What you will learn Range, list and hash partitioning and when to use each Creating partitions and automating them Dropping and detaching old data safely Making queries use partition pruning Declarative partitioning CREATE TABLE events ( id bigint GENERATED ALWAYS AS IDENTITY , created_at timestamptz NOT NULL , kind text NOT NULL , payload jsonb ) PARTITION BY RANGE (created_at); CREATE TABLE events_2026_11 PARTITION OF events FOR VALUES FROM ( &#x27;2026-11-01&#x27; ) TO ( &#x27;2026-12-01&#x27; ); CREATE INDEX ON events_2026_11 (created_at, kind); Range partitions by time suit logs and events. List partitions split by a category such as region. Hash spreads rows evenly across a fixed number of partitions. Primary keys and unique constraints must include the partition key. Automate and retire Create next month&#39;s partition before it is needed, with a scheduled job or an extension, and alert if it is missing, because inserts outside every range fail. Retire old data by detaching, archiving and dropping: ALTER TABLE events DETACH PARTITION events_2025_11 CONCURRENTLY; -- archive with pg_dump if required, then: DROP TABLE events_2025_11; Dropping a partition is fast and does not create dead rows, unlike deleting millions of rows. Pruning EXPLAIN SELECT count ( * ) FROM events WHERE created_at >= &#x27;2026-11-01&#x27; AND created_at \u003c &#x27;2026-11-08&#x27; ; If the plan shows only the relevant partitions, pruning works. Queries that omit the partition key scan all partitions. Keep the number of partitions reasonable, because thousands of tiny partitions slow planning. How the parts fit together The lesson in one picture: each box is a section of this lesson, in the order you meet it. Select a box to see what it does and the mistake that usually goes with it. How Partitioning and data retention fits together Flow in order: 1, Declarative partitioning to Automate and retire; 2, Automate and retire to Pruning. Step Numbered arrows show the order of the flow Select a component to see what it does and what happens when it fails. Declarative partitioning Step What it does: Range partitions by time suit logs and events. If it fails or is weak: Forgetting to create future partitions. Automate and retire Step What it does: Create next month's partition before it is needed, with a scheduled job or an extension, and alert if it is missing, because inserts outside every range fail. If it fails or is weak: Deleting rows instead of dropping partitions. Pruning Step What it does: If the plan shows only the relevant partitions, pruning works. If it fails or is weak: Queries that do not use the partition key. Incident: No partition for today Incident Inserts failed at midnight because the next partition did not exist. ERROR: no partition of relation \"events\" found for row DETAIL: Partition key of the failing row contains (created_at) = (2026-12-01 00:00:03+00). The job that created monthly partitions had been failing silently for weeks. At the month boundary writes began to fail. The team added a default partition as a safety net, alerts for missing future partitions and a test that checks three months ahead. Common mistakes Forgetting to create future partitions Deleting rows instead of dropping partitions Queries that do not use the partition key Creating thousands of tiny partitions"},"sql-ops/sql-backup-and-point-in-time-recovery-operations":{"html":"\u003cp>Point-in-time recovery lets you undo mistakes, not just survive disk loss. It needs a base backup, a continuous stream of write-ahead log files and a practiced procedure. This lesson shows the moving parts and how to test them.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"what-you-will-learn\">What you will learn\u003c/h2>\n\u003cul>\n\u003cli>How base backups and WAL archiving work together\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Restoring to a specific time on a new server\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Measuring RPO and RTO\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Monitoring and testing the backup system\u003c/li>\n\u003c/ul>\n\u003ch2 id=\"the-pieces\">The pieces\u003c/h2>\n\u003cp>A \u003cstrong>base backup\u003c/strong> is a full copy of the data directory at one moment. \u003cstrong>WAL archiving\u003c/strong> copies every write-ahead log segment to safe storage. To recover, restore the base backup and replay WAL up to a target time, transaction or label.\u003c/p>\n\u003cdiv class=\"code\" data-lang=\"bash\">\u003cbutton class=\"copy\" type=\"button\" data-copy=\"# with pgBackRest\npgbackrest --stanza=main backup --type=full\npgbackrest --stanza=main info\" aria-label=\"Copy code\">Copy\u003c/button>\u003cpre tabindex=\"0\">\u003ccode class=\"hljs\">\u003cspan class=\"hljs-comment\"># with pgBackRest\u003c/span>\npgbackrest --stanza=main backup --\u003cspan class=\"hljs-built_in\">type\u003c/span>=full\npgbackrest --stanza=main info\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>\n\u003cdiv class=\"code\" data-lang=\"text\">\u003cbutton class=\"copy\" type=\"button\" data-copy=\"# postgresql.conf\narchive_mode = on\narchive_command = 'pgbackrest --stanza=main archive-push %p'\" aria-label=\"Copy code\">Copy\u003c/button>\u003cpre tabindex=\"0\">\u003ccode class=\"hljs\"># postgresql.conf\narchive_mode = on\narchive_command = &#x27;pgbackrest --stanza=main archive-push %p&#x27;\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>\n\u003ch2 id=\"restore-to-a-point-in-time\">Restore to a point in time\u003c/h2>\n\u003cdiv class=\"code\" data-lang=\"bash\">\u003cbutton class=\"copy\" type=\"button\" data-copy=\"# on a NEW server, stopped PostgreSQL\npgbackrest --stanza=main --type=time \\\n  --target=&quot;2026-11-05 14:02:00+00&quot; \\\n  --target-action=promote restore\nsystemctl start postgresql\npsql -c &quot;SELECT count(*) FROM invoices;&quot;\" aria-label=\"Copy code\">Copy\u003c/button>\u003cpre tabindex=\"0\">\u003ccode class=\"hljs\">\u003cspan class=\"hljs-comment\"># on a NEW server, stopped PostgreSQL\u003c/span>\npgbackrest --stanza=main --\u003cspan class=\"hljs-built_in\">type\u003c/span>=\u003cspan class=\"hljs-keyword\">time\u003c/span> \\\n  --target=\u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">&quot;2026-11-05 14:02:00+00&quot;\u003c/span> \\\n  --target-action=promote restore\nsystemctl start postgresql\npsql -c \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">&quot;SELECT count(*) FROM invoices;&quot;\u003c/span>\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>\n\u003cp>Never restore over the live server. Restore alongside, verify the data, then export what you need or switch traffic after careful checks.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"rpo-and-rto\">RPO and RTO\u003c/h2>\n\u003cp>\u003cstrong>RPO\u003c/strong> is how much data you can lose, bounded by how far behind WAL archiving can lag. \u003cstrong>RTO\u003c/strong> is how long recovery takes, driven by backup size, network speed and replay time. Measure both in a real restore, and write the numbers in the runbook.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"keep-it-healthy\">Keep it healthy\u003c/h2>\n\u003cp>Alert when \u003ccode>pg_stat_archiver\u003c/code> shows failures or when the newest archived WAL is old. Store backups in a different failure domain, encrypt them, keep retention long enough to catch slow-moving mistakes and run a full restore test every month.\u003c/p>\n\u003cdiv class=\"code\" data-lang=\"sql\">\u003cbutton class=\"copy\" type=\"button\" data-copy=\"SELECT last_archived_time, failed_count, last_failed_time FROM pg_stat_archiver;\" aria-label=\"Copy code\">Copy\u003c/button>\u003cpre tabindex=\"0\">\u003ccode class=\"hljs\">\u003cspan class=\"hljs-keyword\">SELECT\u003c/span> last_archived_time, failed_count, last_failed_time \u003cspan class=\"hljs-keyword\">FROM\u003c/span> pg_stat_archiver;\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>\n\u003ch2 id=\"how-the-parts-fit-together\">How the parts fit together\u003c/h2>\n\u003cp>The lesson in one picture: each box is a section of this lesson, in the order you meet it. Select a box to see what it does and the mistake that usually goes with it.\u003c/p>\n\u003cfigure class=\"dg\" data-rows=\"2\" data-edges=\"n1&gt;n2;n2&gt;n3;n3&gt;n4\" style=\"--rows:2\">\u003cfigcaption class=\"dg-title\">How Backup and point-in-time recovery operations for... fits together\u003c/figcaption>\u003cdiv class=\"dg-stage\">\u003csvg viewBox=\"0 0 100 100\" preserveAspectRatio=\"none\" aria-hidden=\"true\" focusable=\"false\">\u003cline x1=\"12.5\" y1=\"25.0\" x2=\"37.5\" y2=\"75.0\"/>\u003cline x1=\"37.5\" y1=\"75.0\" x2=\"62.5\" y2=\"25.0\"/>\u003cline x1=\"62.5\" y1=\"25.0\" x2=\"87.5\" y2=\"75.0\"/>\u003c/svg>\u003cbutton type=\"button\" class=\"dg-node k-process\" data-act=\"dg-pick\" data-id=\"n1\" data-kind=\"process\" style=\"left:12.5%;top:25.0%;--w:22.0%\" aria-pressed=\"false\">\u003cspan class=\"dg-name\">The pieces\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"dg-kind\">Step\u003c/span>\u003c/button>\u003cbutton type=\"button\" class=\"dg-node k-store\" data-act=\"dg-pick\" data-id=\"n2\" data-kind=\"store\" style=\"left:37.5%;top:75.0%;--w:22.0%\" aria-pressed=\"false\">\u003cspan class=\"dg-name\">Restore to a point in time\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"dg-kind\">Data store\u003c/span>\u003c/button>\u003cbutton type=\"button\" class=\"dg-node k-process\" data-act=\"dg-pick\" data-id=\"n3\" data-kind=\"process\" style=\"left:62.5%;top:25.0%;--w:22.0%\" aria-pressed=\"false\">\u003cspan class=\"dg-name\">RPO and RTO\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"dg-kind\">Step\u003c/span>\u003c/button>\u003cbutton type=\"button\" class=\"dg-node k-process\" data-act=\"dg-pick\" data-id=\"n4\" data-kind=\"process\" style=\"left:87.5%;top:75.0%;--w:22.0%\" aria-pressed=\"false\">\u003cspan class=\"dg-name\">Keep it healthy\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"dg-kind\">Step\u003c/span>\u003c/button>\u003c/div>\u003cp class=\"sr\">Flow in order: 1, The pieces to Restore to a point in time; 2, Restore to a point in time to RPO and RTO; 3, RPO and RTO to Keep it healthy.\u003c/p>\u003cul class=\"dg-legend\" aria-label=\"Legend\">\u003cli>\u003ci class=\"dg-sw k-process\" aria-hidden=\"true\">\u003c/i>Step\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003ci class=\"dg-sw k-store\" aria-hidden=\"true\">\u003c/i>Data store\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003ci class=\"dg-arrow\" aria-hidden=\"true\">\u003c/i>Numbered arrows show the order of the flow\u003c/li>\u003c/ul>\u003cp class=\"dg-hint small\">Select a component to see what it does and what happens when it fails. \u003cbutton type=\"button\" class=\"btn btn-quiet dg-flow-btn\" data-act=\"dg-flow\" aria-pressed=\"false\">Show the flow\u003c/button>\u003c/p>\u003cdiv class=\"dg-live\" role=\"status\" aria-live=\"polite\">\u003c/div>\u003cdl class=\"dg-list\">\u003cdiv class=\"dg-item\" data-id=\"n1\">\u003cdt>The pieces \u003cspan class=\"dg-kt\">Step\u003c/span>\u003c/dt>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">What it does:\u003c/span> A base backup is a full copy of the data directory at one moment.\u003c/dd>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">If it fails or is weak:\u003c/span> Never testing a full restore.\u003c/dd>\u003c/div>\u003cdiv class=\"dg-item\" data-id=\"n2\">\u003cdt>Restore to a point in time \u003cspan class=\"dg-kt\">Data store\u003c/span>\u003c/dt>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">What it does:\u003c/span> Never restore over the live server. Restore alongside, verify the data, then export what you need or switch traffic after careful checks.\u003c/dd>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">If it fails or is weak:\u003c/span> Restoring over the live server.\u003c/dd>\u003c/div>\u003cdiv class=\"dg-item\" data-id=\"n3\">\u003cdt>RPO and RTO \u003cspan class=\"dg-kt\">Step\u003c/span>\u003c/dt>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">What it does:\u003c/span> RPO is how much data you can lose, bounded by how far behind WAL archiving can lag.\u003c/dd>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">If it fails or is weak:\u003c/span> Check the Common mistakes list below this diagram for what usually goes wrong here.\u003c/dd>\u003c/div>\u003cdiv class=\"dg-item\" data-id=\"n4\">\u003cdt>Keep it healthy \u003cspan class=\"dg-kt\">Step\u003c/span>\u003c/dt>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">What it does:\u003c/span> Alert when pg_stat_archiver shows failures or when the newest archived WAL is old.\u003c/dd>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">If it fails or is weak:\u003c/span> Backups stored on the same failure domain.\u003c/dd>\u003c/div>\u003c/dl>\u003c/figure>\n\u003ch2 id=\"incident-archiving-had-been-failing-for-nine-days\">Incident: Archiving had been failing for nine days\u003c/h2>\n\u003caside class=\"callout callout-incident\">\u003cdiv class=\"callout-title\">Incident\u003c/div>\u003cp>A restore needed WAL that was never archived.\u003c/p>\n\u003c/aside>\n\u003cdiv class=\"code console\" data-lang=\"terminal\">\u003cpre tabindex=\"0\">\u003ccode class=\"hljs\">\u003cspan class=\"ln out\">FATAL:  could not locate required checkpoint record\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln out\">pg_stat_archiver: failed_count = 11204\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln out\">last_failed_time: 9 days ago\u003c/span>\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>\n\u003cp>A full disk on the archive target had stopped copies, but the base backup job still reported success. When recovery was needed the log chain was broken. The team alerts on archiver failures and lag, monitors the target and runs a monthly restore test that must reach a given time.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"common-mistakes\">Common mistakes\u003c/h2>\n\u003cul>\n\u003cli>Never testing a full restore\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Restoring over the live server\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Backups stored on the same failure domain\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Ignoring WAL archive failures\u003c/li>\n\u003c/ul>\n","toc":[{"id":"what-you-will-learn","text":"What you will learn"},{"id":"the-pieces","text":"The pieces"},{"id":"restore-to-a-point-in-time","text":"Restore to a point in time"},{"id":"rpo-and-rto","text":"RPO and RTO"},{"id":"keep-it-healthy","text":"Keep it healthy"},{"id":"how-the-parts-fit-together","text":"How the parts fit together"},{"id":"incident-archiving-had-been-failing-for-nine-days","text":"Incident: Archiving had been failing for nine days"},{"id":"common-mistakes","text":"Common mistakes"}],"refs":[{"t":"PostgreSQL continuous archiving and PITR","u":"https://www.postgresql.org/docs/current/continuous-archiving.html"},{"t":"pgBackRest user guide","u":"https://pgbackrest.org/user-guide.html"}],"text":"Point-in-time recovery lets you undo mistakes, not just survive disk loss. It needs a base backup, a continuous stream of write-ahead log files and a practiced procedure. This lesson shows the moving parts and how to test them. What you will learn How base backups and WAL archiving work together Restoring to a specific time on a new server Measuring RPO and RTO Monitoring and testing the backup system The pieces A base backup is a full copy of the data directory at one moment. WAL archiving copies every write-ahead log segment to safe storage. To recover, restore the base backup and replay WAL up to a target time, transaction or label. # with pgBackRest pgbackrest --stanza=main backup -- type =full pgbackrest --stanza=main info # postgresql.conf archive_mode = on archive_command = &#x27;pgbackrest --stanza=main archive-push %p&#x27; Restore to a point in time # on a NEW server, stopped PostgreSQL pgbackrest --stanza=main -- type = time \\ --target= \"2026-11-05 14:02:00+00\" \\ --target-action=promote restore systemctl start postgresql psql -c \"SELECT count(*) FROM invoices;\" Never restore over the live server. Restore alongside, verify the data, then export what you need or switch traffic after careful checks. RPO and RTO RPO is how much data you can lose, bounded by how far behind WAL archiving can lag. RTO is how long recovery takes, driven by backup size, network speed and replay time. Measure both in a real restore, and write the numbers in the runbook. Keep it healthy Alert when pg_stat_archiver shows failures or when the newest archived WAL is old. Store backups in a different failure domain, encrypt them, keep retention long enough to catch slow-moving mistakes and run a full restore test every month. SELECT last_archived_time, failed_count, last_failed_time FROM pg_stat_archiver; How the parts fit together The lesson in one picture: each box is a section of this lesson, in the order you meet it. Select a box to see what it does and the mistake that usually goes with it. How Backup and point-in-time recovery operations for... fits together Flow in order: 1, The pieces to Restore to a point in time; 2, Restore to a point in time to RPO and RTO; 3, RPO and RTO to Keep it healthy. Step Data store Numbered arrows show the order of the flow Select a component to see what it does and what happens when it fails. The pieces Step What it does: A base backup is a full copy of the data directory at one moment. If it fails or is weak: Never testing a full restore. Restore to a point in time Data store What it does: Never restore over the live server. Restore alongside, verify the data, then export what you need or switch traffic after careful checks. If it fails or is weak: Restoring over the live server. RPO and RTO Step What it does: RPO is how much data you can lose, bounded by how far behind WAL archiving can lag. If it fails or is weak: Check the Common mistakes list below this diagram for what usually goes wrong here. Keep it healthy Step What it does: Alert when pg_stat_archiver shows failures or when the newest archived WAL is old. If it fails or is weak: Backups stored on the same failure domain. Incident: Archiving had been failing for nine days Incident A restore needed WAL that was never archived. FATAL: could not locate required checkpoint record pg_stat_archiver: failed_count = 11204 last_failed_time: 9 days ago A full disk on the archive target had stopped copies, but the base backup job still reported success. When recovery was needed the log chain was broken. The team alerts on archiver failures and lag, monitors the target and runs a monthly restore test that must reach a given time. Common mistakes Never testing a full restore Restoring over the live server Backups stored on the same failure domain Ignoring WAL archive failures"},"sql-ops/online-schema-changes-without-downtime":{"html":"\u003cp>Schema changes are routine but dangerous on a live system. The risk is rarely the work itself; it is the lock the statement needs and the queue of queries that forms behind it. A handful of habits make changes safe.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"what-you-will-learn\">What you will learn\u003c/h2>\n\u003cul>\n\u003cli>Which operations take strong locks\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Using lock_timeout and CONCURRENTLY\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Backfilling data in batches\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Adding constraints without blocking writes\u003c/li>\n\u003c/ul>\n\u003ch2 id=\"locks-and-timeouts\">Locks and timeouts\u003c/h2>\n\u003cdiv class=\"code\" data-lang=\"sql\">\u003cbutton class=\"copy\" type=\"button\" data-copy=\"SET lock_timeout = '3s';\nSET statement_timeout = '15min';\n\nALTER TABLE orders ADD COLUMN channel text;           -- fast, no rewrite\nCREATE INDEX CONCURRENTLY orders_channel_idx ON orders (channel);\" aria-label=\"Copy code\">Copy\u003c/button>\u003cpre tabindex=\"0\">\u003ccode class=\"hljs\">\u003cspan class=\"hljs-keyword\">SET\u003c/span> lock_timeout \u003cspan class=\"hljs-operator\">=\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">&#x27;3s&#x27;\u003c/span>;\n\u003cspan class=\"hljs-keyword\">SET\u003c/span> statement_timeout \u003cspan class=\"hljs-operator\">=\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">&#x27;15min&#x27;\u003c/span>;\n\n\u003cspan class=\"hljs-keyword\">ALTER TABLE\u003c/span> orders \u003cspan class=\"hljs-keyword\">ADD\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-keyword\">COLUMN\u003c/span> channel text;           \u003cspan class=\"hljs-comment\">-- fast, no rewrite\u003c/span>\n\u003cspan class=\"hljs-keyword\">CREATE\u003c/span> INDEX CONCURRENTLY orders_channel_idx \u003cspan class=\"hljs-keyword\">ON\u003c/span> orders (channel);\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>\n\u003cp>\u003ccode>ALTER TABLE\u003c/code> needs a strong lock briefly, but if a long transaction holds a conflicting lock it waits, and every later query waits behind it. A \u003cstrong>lock_timeout\u003c/strong> makes the migration fail fast so you can retry in a quieter moment. \u003ccode>CREATE INDEX CONCURRENTLY\u003c/code> avoids blocking writes but takes longer and cannot run inside a transaction.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"backfill-in-batches\">Backfill in batches\u003c/h2>\n\u003cdiv class=\"code\" data-lang=\"sql\">\u003cbutton class=\"copy\" type=\"button\" data-copy=\"-- repeat until 0 rows are updated\nUPDATE orders SET channel = 'web'\nWHERE id IN (SELECT id FROM orders WHERE channel IS NULL LIMIT 5000);\" aria-label=\"Copy code\">Copy\u003c/button>\u003cpre tabindex=\"0\">\u003ccode class=\"hljs\">\u003cspan class=\"hljs-comment\">-- repeat until 0 rows are updated\u003c/span>\n\u003cspan class=\"hljs-keyword\">UPDATE\u003c/span> orders \u003cspan class=\"hljs-keyword\">SET\u003c/span> channel \u003cspan class=\"hljs-operator\">=\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">&#x27;web&#x27;\u003c/span>\n\u003cspan class=\"hljs-keyword\">WHERE\u003c/span> id \u003cspan class=\"hljs-keyword\">IN\u003c/span> (\u003cspan class=\"hljs-keyword\">SELECT\u003c/span> id \u003cspan class=\"hljs-keyword\">FROM\u003c/span> orders \u003cspan class=\"hljs-keyword\">WHERE\u003c/span> channel \u003cspan class=\"hljs-keyword\">IS\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-keyword\">NULL\u003c/span> LIMIT \u003cspan class=\"hljs-number\">5000\u003c/span>);\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>\n\u003cp>One huge update holds locks, bloats the table and creates replication lag. Small batches with a short pause let vacuum and replicas keep up. Watch lag while it runs.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"constraints\">Constraints\u003c/h2>\n\u003cdiv class=\"code\" data-lang=\"sql\">\u003cbutton class=\"copy\" type=\"button\" data-copy=\"ALTER TABLE orders ADD CONSTRAINT orders_channel_chk\n  CHECK (channel IS NOT NULL) NOT VALID;\nALTER TABLE orders VALIDATE CONSTRAINT orders_channel_chk;\" aria-label=\"Copy code\">Copy\u003c/button>\u003cpre tabindex=\"0\">\u003ccode class=\"hljs\">\u003cspan class=\"hljs-keyword\">ALTER TABLE\u003c/span> orders \u003cspan class=\"hljs-keyword\">ADD CONSTRAINT\u003c/span> orders_channel_chk\n  \u003cspan class=\"hljs-keyword\">CHECK\u003c/span> (channel \u003cspan class=\"hljs-keyword\">IS\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-keyword\">NOT NULL\u003c/span>) \u003cspan class=\"hljs-keyword\">NOT\u003c/span> VALID;\n\u003cspan class=\"hljs-keyword\">ALTER TABLE\u003c/span> orders VALIDATE \u003cspan class=\"hljs-keyword\">CONSTRAINT\u003c/span> orders_channel_chk;\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>\n\u003cp>Adding a constraint as \u003ccode>NOT VALID\u003c/code> checks only new rows and needs a short lock. \u003ccode>VALIDATE\u003c/code> then scans existing rows with a weaker lock. The same idea applies to foreign keys.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"expand-and-contract\">Expand and contract\u003c/h2>\n\u003cp>Make changes compatible in steps: \u003cstrong>expand\u003c/strong> by adding the new column, deploy code that writes both, backfill, switch reads, then \u003cstrong>contract\u003c/strong> by removing the old column in a later release. Each step can be rolled back.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"how-the-parts-fit-together\">How the parts fit together\u003c/h2>\n\u003cp>The lesson in one picture: each box is a section of this lesson, in the order you meet it. Select a box to see what it does and the mistake that usually goes with it.\u003c/p>\n\u003cfigure class=\"dg\" data-rows=\"2\" data-edges=\"n1&gt;n2;n2&gt;n3;n3&gt;n4\" style=\"--rows:2\">\u003cfigcaption class=\"dg-title\">How Online schema changes without downtime fits together\u003c/figcaption>\u003cdiv class=\"dg-stage\">\u003csvg viewBox=\"0 0 100 100\" preserveAspectRatio=\"none\" aria-hidden=\"true\" focusable=\"false\">\u003cline x1=\"12.5\" y1=\"25.0\" x2=\"37.5\" y2=\"75.0\"/>\u003cline x1=\"37.5\" y1=\"75.0\" x2=\"62.5\" y2=\"25.0\"/>\u003cline x1=\"62.5\" y1=\"25.0\" x2=\"87.5\" y2=\"75.0\"/>\u003c/svg>\u003cbutton type=\"button\" class=\"dg-node k-process\" data-act=\"dg-pick\" data-id=\"n1\" data-kind=\"process\" style=\"left:12.5%;top:25.0%;--w:22.0%\" aria-pressed=\"false\">\u003cspan class=\"dg-name\">Locks and timeouts\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"dg-kind\">Step\u003c/span>\u003c/button>\u003cbutton type=\"button\" class=\"dg-node k-process\" data-act=\"dg-pick\" data-id=\"n2\" data-kind=\"process\" style=\"left:37.5%;top:75.0%;--w:22.0%\" aria-pressed=\"false\">\u003cspan class=\"dg-name\">Backfill in batches\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"dg-kind\">Step\u003c/span>\u003c/button>\u003cbutton type=\"button\" class=\"dg-node k-process\" data-act=\"dg-pick\" data-id=\"n3\" data-kind=\"process\" style=\"left:62.5%;top:25.0%;--w:22.0%\" aria-pressed=\"false\">\u003cspan class=\"dg-name\">Constraints\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"dg-kind\">Step\u003c/span>\u003c/button>\u003cbutton type=\"button\" class=\"dg-node k-process\" data-act=\"dg-pick\" data-id=\"n4\" data-kind=\"process\" style=\"left:87.5%;top:75.0%;--w:22.0%\" aria-pressed=\"false\">\u003cspan class=\"dg-name\">Expand and contract\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"dg-kind\">Step\u003c/span>\u003c/button>\u003c/div>\u003cp class=\"sr\">Flow in order: 1, Locks and timeouts to Backfill in batches; 2, Backfill in batches to Constraints; 3, Constraints to Expand and contract.\u003c/p>\u003cul class=\"dg-legend\" aria-label=\"Legend\">\u003cli>\u003ci class=\"dg-sw k-process\" aria-hidden=\"true\">\u003c/i>Step\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003ci class=\"dg-arrow\" aria-hidden=\"true\">\u003c/i>Numbered arrows show the order of the flow\u003c/li>\u003c/ul>\u003cp class=\"dg-hint small\">Select a component to see what it does and what happens when it fails. \u003cbutton type=\"button\" class=\"btn btn-quiet dg-flow-btn\" data-act=\"dg-flow\" aria-pressed=\"false\">Show the flow\u003c/button>\u003c/p>\u003cdiv class=\"dg-live\" role=\"status\" aria-live=\"polite\">\u003c/div>\u003cdl class=\"dg-list\">\u003cdiv class=\"dg-item\" data-id=\"n1\">\u003cdt>Locks and timeouts \u003cspan class=\"dg-kt\">Step\u003c/span>\u003c/dt>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">What it does:\u003c/span> ALTER TABLE needs a strong lock briefly, but if a long transaction holds a conflicting lock it waits, and every later query waits behind it.\u003c/dd>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">If it fails or is weak:\u003c/span> Changing hot tables without lock_timeout.\u003c/dd>\u003c/div>\u003cdiv class=\"dg-item\" data-id=\"n2\">\u003cdt>Backfill in batches \u003cspan class=\"dg-kt\">Step\u003c/span>\u003c/dt>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">What it does:\u003c/span> One huge update holds locks, bloats the table and creates replication lag.\u003c/dd>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">If it fails or is weak:\u003c/span> One giant backfill update.\u003c/dd>\u003c/div>\u003cdiv class=\"dg-item\" data-id=\"n3\">\u003cdt>Constraints \u003cspan class=\"dg-kt\">Step\u003c/span>\u003c/dt>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">What it does:\u003c/span> Adding a constraint as NOT VALID checks only new rows and needs a short lock.\u003c/dd>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">If it fails or is weak:\u003c/span> Adding constraints with full validation inline.\u003c/dd>\u003c/div>\u003cdiv class=\"dg-item\" data-id=\"n4\">\u003cdt>Expand and contract \u003cspan class=\"dg-kt\">Step\u003c/span>\u003c/dt>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">What it does:\u003c/span> Make changes compatible in steps: expand by adding the new column, deploy code that writes both, backfill, switch reads, then contract by removing the old column in a...\u003c/dd>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">If it fails or is weak:\u003c/span> Dropping a column in the same release as the code change.\u003c/dd>\u003c/div>\u003c/dl>\u003c/figure>\n\u003ch2 id=\"incident-the-index-that-blocked-all-writes\">Incident: The index that blocked all writes\u003c/h2>\n\u003caside class=\"callout callout-incident\">\u003cdiv class=\"callout-title\">Incident\u003c/div>\u003cp>A plain CREATE INDEX stalled writes on a hot table.\u003c/p>\n\u003c/aside>\n\u003cdiv class=\"code console\" data-lang=\"terminal\">\u003cpre tabindex=\"0\">\u003ccode class=\"hljs\">\u003cspan class=\"ln out\">CREATE INDEX orders_status_idx ON orders (status);\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln out\">waiting for ShareLock on relation orders\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln out\">412 queries waiting, longest 241s\u003c/span>\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>\n\u003cp>A plain index build takes a lock that blocks writes for the whole build. On a large table that was minutes of failed requests. The team standardized on CONCURRENTLY with a lock timeout, added a migration review checklist and ran a rehearsal against a copy of production.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"common-mistakes\">Common mistakes\u003c/h2>\n\u003cul>\n\u003cli>Changing hot tables without lock_timeout\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>One giant backfill update\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Adding constraints with full validation inline\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Dropping a column in the same release as the code change\u003c/li>\n\u003c/ul>\n","toc":[{"id":"what-you-will-learn","text":"What you will learn"},{"id":"locks-and-timeouts","text":"Locks and timeouts"},{"id":"backfill-in-batches","text":"Backfill in batches"},{"id":"constraints","text":"Constraints"},{"id":"expand-and-contract","text":"Expand and contract"},{"id":"how-the-parts-fit-together","text":"How the parts fit together"},{"id":"incident-the-index-that-blocked-all-writes","text":"Incident: The index that blocked all writes"},{"id":"common-mistakes","text":"Common mistakes"}],"refs":[{"t":"PostgreSQL ALTER TABLE","u":"https://www.postgresql.org/docs/current/sql-altertable.html"},{"t":"PostgreSQL explicit locking","u":"https://www.postgresql.org/docs/current/explicit-locking.html"}],"text":"Schema changes are routine but dangerous on a live system. The risk is rarely the work itself; it is the lock the statement needs and the queue of queries that forms behind it. A handful of habits make changes safe. What you will learn Which operations take strong locks Using lock_timeout and CONCURRENTLY Backfilling data in batches Adding constraints without blocking writes Locks and timeouts SET lock_timeout = &#x27;3s&#x27; ; SET statement_timeout = &#x27;15min&#x27; ; ALTER TABLE orders ADD COLUMN channel text; -- fast, no rewrite CREATE INDEX CONCURRENTLY orders_channel_idx ON orders (channel); ALTER TABLE needs a strong lock briefly, but if a long transaction holds a conflicting lock it waits, and every later query waits behind it. A lock_timeout makes the migration fail fast so you can retry in a quieter moment. CREATE INDEX CONCURRENTLY avoids blocking writes but takes longer and cannot run inside a transaction. Backfill in batches -- repeat until 0 rows are updated UPDATE orders SET channel = &#x27;web&#x27; WHERE id IN ( SELECT id FROM orders WHERE channel IS NULL LIMIT 5000 ); One huge update holds locks, bloats the table and creates replication lag. Small batches with a short pause let vacuum and replicas keep up. Watch lag while it runs. Constraints ALTER TABLE orders ADD CONSTRAINT orders_channel_chk CHECK (channel IS NOT NULL ) NOT VALID; ALTER TABLE orders VALIDATE CONSTRAINT orders_channel_chk; Adding a constraint as NOT VALID checks only new rows and needs a short lock. VALIDATE then scans existing rows with a weaker lock. The same idea applies to foreign keys. Expand and contract Make changes compatible in steps: expand by adding the new column, deploy code that writes both, backfill, switch reads, then contract by removing the old column in a later release. Each step can be rolled back. How the parts fit together The lesson in one picture: each box is a section of this lesson, in the order you meet it. Select a box to see what it does and the mistake that usually goes with it. How Online schema changes without downtime fits together Flow in order: 1, Locks and timeouts to Backfill in batches; 2, Backfill in batches to Constraints; 3, Constraints to Expand and contract. Step Numbered arrows show the order of the flow Select a component to see what it does and what happens when it fails. Locks and timeouts Step What it does: ALTER TABLE needs a strong lock briefly, but if a long transaction holds a conflicting lock it waits, and every later query waits behind it. If it fails or is weak: Changing hot tables without lock_timeout. Backfill in batches Step What it does: One huge update holds locks, bloats the table and creates replication lag. If it fails or is weak: One giant backfill update. Constraints Step What it does: Adding a constraint as NOT VALID checks only new rows and needs a short lock. If it fails or is weak: Adding constraints with full validation inline. Expand and contract Step What it does: Make changes compatible in steps: expand by adding the new column, deploy code that writes both, backfill, switch reads, then contract by removing the old column in a... If it fails or is weak: Dropping a column in the same release as the code change. Incident: The index that blocked all writes Incident A plain CREATE INDEX stalled writes on a hot table. CREATE INDEX orders_status_idx ON orders (status); waiting for ShareLock on relation orders 412 queries waiting, longest 241s A plain index build takes a lock that blocks writes for the whole build. On a large table that was minutes of failed requests. The team standardized on CONCURRENTLY with a lock timeout, added a migration review checklist and ran a rehearsal against a copy of production. Common mistakes Changing hot tables without lock_timeout One giant backfill update Adding constraints with full validation inline Dropping a column in the same release as the code change"},"sql-ops/replicas-failover-and-switchover-operations":{"html":"\u003cp>Replicas give you read capacity and a way to survive a lost server. The hard part is the moment of change: promoting a replica safely without two servers believing they are the primary.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"what-you-will-learn\">What you will learn\u003c/h2>\n\u003cul>\n\u003cli>Streaming replication and measuring lag\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Planned switchover versus emergency failover\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Split brain and fencing\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Rebuilding a failed primary\u003c/li>\n\u003c/ul>\n\u003ch2 id=\"replication-and-lag\">Replication and lag\u003c/h2>\n\u003cdiv class=\"code\" data-lang=\"sql\">\u003cbutton class=\"copy\" type=\"button\" data-copy=\"-- on the primary\nSELECT client_addr, state, sent_lsn, replay_lsn,\n       pg_wal_lsn_diff(sent_lsn, replay_lsn) AS lag_bytes\nFROM pg_stat_replication;\n\n-- on a replica\nSELECT now() - pg_last_xact_replay_timestamp() AS replay_delay;\" aria-label=\"Copy code\">Copy\u003c/button>\u003cpre tabindex=\"0\">\u003ccode class=\"hljs\">\u003cspan class=\"hljs-comment\">-- on the primary\u003c/span>\n\u003cspan class=\"hljs-keyword\">SELECT\u003c/span> client_addr, state, sent_lsn, replay_lsn,\n       pg_wal_lsn_diff(sent_lsn, replay_lsn) \u003cspan class=\"hljs-keyword\">AS\u003c/span> lag_bytes\n\u003cspan class=\"hljs-keyword\">FROM\u003c/span> pg_stat_replication;\n\n\u003cspan class=\"hljs-comment\">-- on a replica\u003c/span>\n\u003cspan class=\"hljs-keyword\">SELECT\u003c/span> now() \u003cspan class=\"hljs-operator\">-\u003c/span> pg_last_xact_replay_timestamp() \u003cspan class=\"hljs-keyword\">AS\u003c/span> replay_delay;\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>\n\u003cp>Lag grows when replicas are slow, the network is busy or a long query on the replica conflicts with replay. Alert on both bytes and seconds. Reads from a lagging replica can return old data, so route read-your-own-write traffic to the primary.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"switchover-and-failover\">Switchover and failover\u003c/h2>\n\u003cp>A \u003cstrong>switchover\u003c/strong> is planned: stop writes, let the replica catch up fully, promote it and demote the old primary with no data loss. A \u003cstrong>failover\u003c/strong> is unplanned: the primary is gone, you promote the most up-to-date replica and accept that some recent transactions may be lost if replication was asynchronous.\u003c/p>\n\u003cdiv class=\"code\" data-lang=\"bash\">\u003cbutton class=\"copy\" type=\"button\" data-copy=\"patronictl -c /etc/patroni.yml list\npatronictl -c /etc/patroni.yml switchover --candidate db2 --scheduled now\" aria-label=\"Copy code\">Copy\u003c/button>\u003cpre tabindex=\"0\">\u003ccode class=\"hljs\">patronictl -c /etc/patroni.yml list\npatronictl -c /etc/patroni.yml switchover --candidate db2 --scheduled now\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>\n\u003cp>Synchronous replication reduces data loss at the price of write latency and availability if the replica is down.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"avoiding-split-brain\">Avoiding split brain\u003c/h2>\n\u003cp>Use a \u003cstrong>consensus\u003c/strong> store and \u003cstrong>fencing\u003c/strong>: the old primary must be unable to accept writes before another is promoted, for example by losing its leader lock, being shut down or being cut off at the network layer. Clients should connect through a virtual address, proxy or DNS name that follows the leader.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"rebuild\">Rebuild\u003c/h2>\n\u003cp>An old primary may have extra transactions. Rewind it with \u003ccode>pg_rewind\u003c/code> or rebuild it from a base backup, then join it as a replica. Never simply restart it as a primary.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"how-the-parts-fit-together\">How the parts fit together\u003c/h2>\n\u003cp>The lesson in one picture: each box is a section of this lesson, in the order you meet it. Select a box to see what it does and the mistake that usually goes with it.\u003c/p>\n\u003cfigure class=\"dg\" data-rows=\"2\" data-edges=\"n1&gt;n2;n2&gt;n3;n3&gt;n4\" style=\"--rows:2\">\u003cfigcaption class=\"dg-title\">How Replicas, switchover and failover operations fits together\u003c/figcaption>\u003cdiv class=\"dg-stage\">\u003csvg viewBox=\"0 0 100 100\" preserveAspectRatio=\"none\" aria-hidden=\"true\" focusable=\"false\">\u003cline x1=\"12.5\" y1=\"25.0\" x2=\"37.5\" y2=\"75.0\"/>\u003cline x1=\"37.5\" y1=\"75.0\" x2=\"62.5\" y2=\"25.0\"/>\u003cline x1=\"62.5\" y1=\"25.0\" x2=\"87.5\" y2=\"75.0\"/>\u003c/svg>\u003cbutton type=\"button\" class=\"dg-node k-process\" data-act=\"dg-pick\" data-id=\"n1\" data-kind=\"process\" style=\"left:12.5%;top:25.0%;--w:22.0%\" aria-pressed=\"false\">\u003cspan class=\"dg-name\">Replication and lag\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"dg-kind\">Step\u003c/span>\u003c/button>\u003cbutton type=\"button\" class=\"dg-node k-process\" data-act=\"dg-pick\" data-id=\"n2\" data-kind=\"process\" style=\"left:37.5%;top:75.0%;--w:22.0%\" aria-pressed=\"false\">\u003cspan class=\"dg-name\">Switchover and failover\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"dg-kind\">Step\u003c/span>\u003c/button>\u003cbutton type=\"button\" class=\"dg-node k-process\" data-act=\"dg-pick\" data-id=\"n3\" data-kind=\"process\" style=\"left:62.5%;top:25.0%;--w:22.0%\" aria-pressed=\"false\">\u003cspan class=\"dg-name\">Avoiding split brain\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"dg-kind\">Step\u003c/span>\u003c/button>\u003cbutton type=\"button\" class=\"dg-node k-process\" data-act=\"dg-pick\" data-id=\"n4\" data-kind=\"process\" style=\"left:87.5%;top:75.0%;--w:22.0%\" aria-pressed=\"false\">\u003cspan class=\"dg-name\">Rebuild\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"dg-kind\">Step\u003c/span>\u003c/button>\u003c/div>\u003cp class=\"sr\">Flow in order: 1, Replication and lag to Switchover and failover; 2, Switchover and failover to Avoiding split brain; 3, Avoiding split brain to Rebuild.\u003c/p>\u003cul class=\"dg-legend\" aria-label=\"Legend\">\u003cli>\u003ci class=\"dg-sw k-process\" aria-hidden=\"true\">\u003c/i>Step\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003ci class=\"dg-arrow\" aria-hidden=\"true\">\u003c/i>Numbered arrows show the order of the flow\u003c/li>\u003c/ul>\u003cp class=\"dg-hint small\">Select a component to see what it does and what happens when it fails. \u003cbutton type=\"button\" class=\"btn btn-quiet dg-flow-btn\" data-act=\"dg-flow\" aria-pressed=\"false\">Show the flow\u003c/button>\u003c/p>\u003cdiv class=\"dg-live\" role=\"status\" aria-live=\"polite\">\u003c/div>\u003cdl class=\"dg-list\">\u003cdiv class=\"dg-item\" data-id=\"n1\">\u003cdt>Replication and lag \u003cspan class=\"dg-kt\">Step\u003c/span>\u003c/dt>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">What it does:\u003c/span> Lag grows when replicas are slow, the network is busy or a long query on the replica conflicts with replay.\u003c/dd>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">If it fails or is weak:\u003c/span> Ignoring replica lag in application reads.\u003c/dd>\u003c/div>\u003cdiv class=\"dg-item\" data-id=\"n2\">\u003cdt>Switchover and failover \u003cspan class=\"dg-kt\">Step\u003c/span>\u003c/dt>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">What it does:\u003c/span> A switchover is planned: stop writes, let the replica catch up fully, promote it and demote the old primary with no data loss.\u003c/dd>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">If it fails or is weak:\u003c/span> Automatic failover that was never tested.\u003c/dd>\u003c/div>\u003cdiv class=\"dg-item\" data-id=\"n3\">\u003cdt>Avoiding split brain \u003cspan class=\"dg-kt\">Step\u003c/span>\u003c/dt>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">What it does:\u003c/span> Use a consensus store and fencing: the old primary must be unable to accept writes before another is promoted, for example by losing its leader lock, being shut down or...\u003c/dd>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">If it fails or is weak:\u003c/span> Promoting without fencing the old primary.\u003c/dd>\u003c/div>\u003cdiv class=\"dg-item\" data-id=\"n4\">\u003cdt>Rebuild \u003cspan class=\"dg-kt\">Step\u003c/span>\u003c/dt>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">What it does:\u003c/span> An old primary may have extra transactions.\u003c/dd>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">If it fails or is weak:\u003c/span> Restarting a failed primary as a primary.\u003c/dd>\u003c/div>\u003c/dl>\u003c/figure>\n\u003ch2 id=\"incident-two-primaries\">Incident: Two primaries\u003c/h2>\n\u003caside class=\"callout callout-incident\">\u003cdiv class=\"callout-title\">Incident\u003c/div>\u003cp>A partitioned old primary kept accepting writes after promotion.\u003c/p>\n\u003c/aside>\n\u003cdiv class=\"code console\" data-lang=\"terminal\">\u003cpre tabindex=\"0\">\u003ccode class=\"hljs\">\u003cspan class=\"ln out\">db1: still accepting writes (leader lock lost 4m ago)\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln out\">db2: promoted to primary\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln out\">diverged writes: 1,842 rows on db1 only\u003c/span>\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>\n\u003cp>The failover manager promoted db2 but nothing stopped db1 from running. Some application servers kept writing to it. The team enabled a watchdog so a node without the leader lock shuts down its database, moved clients behind a proxy that follows the leader, and wrote a runbook for reconciling data if it happens again.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"common-mistakes\">Common mistakes\u003c/h2>\n\u003cul>\n\u003cli>Promoting without fencing the old primary\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Restarting a failed primary as a primary\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Ignoring replica lag in application reads\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Automatic failover that was never tested\u003c/li>\n\u003c/ul>\n","toc":[{"id":"what-you-will-learn","text":"What you will learn"},{"id":"replication-and-lag","text":"Replication and lag"},{"id":"switchover-and-failover","text":"Switchover and failover"},{"id":"avoiding-split-brain","text":"Avoiding split brain"},{"id":"rebuild","text":"Rebuild"},{"id":"how-the-parts-fit-together","text":"How the parts fit together"},{"id":"incident-two-primaries","text":"Incident: Two primaries"},{"id":"common-mistakes","text":"Common mistakes"}],"refs":[{"t":"PostgreSQL high availability and replication","u":"https://www.postgresql.org/docs/current/high-availability.html"},{"t":"PostgreSQL warm standby and streaming replication","u":"https://www.postgresql.org/docs/current/warm-standby.html"}],"text":"Replicas give you read capacity and a way to survive a lost server. The hard part is the moment of change: promoting a replica safely without two servers believing they are the primary. What you will learn Streaming replication and measuring lag Planned switchover versus emergency failover Split brain and fencing Rebuilding a failed primary Replication and lag -- on the primary SELECT client_addr, state, sent_lsn, replay_lsn, pg_wal_lsn_diff(sent_lsn, replay_lsn) AS lag_bytes FROM pg_stat_replication; -- on a replica SELECT now() - pg_last_xact_replay_timestamp() AS replay_delay; Lag grows when replicas are slow, the network is busy or a long query on the replica conflicts with replay. Alert on both bytes and seconds. Reads from a lagging replica can return old data, so route read-your-own-write traffic to the primary. Switchover and failover A switchover is planned: stop writes, let the replica catch up fully, promote it and demote the old primary with no data loss. A failover is unplanned: the primary is gone, you promote the most up-to-date replica and accept that some recent transactions may be lost if replication was asynchronous. patronictl -c /etc/patroni.yml list patronictl -c /etc/patroni.yml switchover --candidate db2 --scheduled now Synchronous replication reduces data loss at the price of write latency and availability if the replica is down. Avoiding split brain Use a consensus store and fencing : the old primary must be unable to accept writes before another is promoted, for example by losing its leader lock, being shut down or being cut off at the network layer. Clients should connect through a virtual address, proxy or DNS name that follows the leader. Rebuild An old primary may have extra transactions. Rewind it with pg_rewind or rebuild it from a base backup, then join it as a replica. Never simply restart it as a primary. How the parts fit together The lesson in one picture: each box is a section of this lesson, in the order you meet it. Select a box to see what it does and the mistake that usually goes with it. How Replicas, switchover and failover operations fits together Flow in order: 1, Replication and lag to Switchover and failover; 2, Switchover and failover to Avoiding split brain; 3, Avoiding split brain to Rebuild. Step Numbered arrows show the order of the flow Select a component to see what it does and what happens when it fails. Replication and lag Step What it does: Lag grows when replicas are slow, the network is busy or a long query on the replica conflicts with replay. If it fails or is weak: Ignoring replica lag in application reads. Switchover and failover Step What it does: A switchover is planned: stop writes, let the replica catch up fully, promote it and demote the old primary with no data loss. If it fails or is weak: Automatic failover that was never tested. Avoiding split brain Step What it does: Use a consensus store and fencing: the old primary must be unable to accept writes before another is promoted, for example by losing its leader lock, being shut down or... If it fails or is weak: Promoting without fencing the old primary. Rebuild Step What it does: An old primary may have extra transactions. If it fails or is weak: Restarting a failed primary as a primary. Incident: Two primaries Incident A partitioned old primary kept accepting writes after promotion. db1: still accepting writes (leader lock lost 4m ago) db2: promoted to primary diverged writes: 1,842 rows on db1 only The failover manager promoted db2 but nothing stopped db1 from running. Some application servers kept writing to it. The team enabled a watchdog so a node without the leader lock shuts down its database, moved clients behind a proxy that follows the leader, and wrote a runbook for reconciling data if it happens again. Common mistakes Promoting without fencing the old primary Restarting a failed primary as a primary Ignoring replica lag in application reads Automatic failover that was never tested"},"sql-ops/autovacuum-bloat-and-maintenance-windows":{"html":"\u003cp>Updates and deletes in PostgreSQL create new row versions and leave old ones behind. Autovacuum reclaims them in the background. When it cannot keep up, or cannot remove rows because something still needs them, tables bloat and performance drops.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"what-you-will-learn\">What you will learn\u003c/h2>\n\u003cul>\n\u003cli>Dead tuples, bloat and what autovacuum does\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Finding what blocks vacuum\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Tuning autovacuum for busy tables\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Transaction ID wraparound and safe maintenance\u003c/li>\n\u003c/ul>\n\u003ch2 id=\"watch-vacuum\">Watch vacuum\u003c/h2>\n\u003cdiv class=\"code\" data-lang=\"sql\">\u003cbutton class=\"copy\" type=\"button\" data-copy=\"SELECT relname, n_live_tup, n_dead_tup,\n       last_autovacuum, last_autoanalyze\nFROM pg_stat_user_tables\nORDER BY n_dead_tup DESC LIMIT 10;\" aria-label=\"Copy code\">Copy\u003c/button>\u003cpre tabindex=\"0\">\u003ccode class=\"hljs\">\u003cspan class=\"hljs-keyword\">SELECT\u003c/span> relname, n_live_tup, n_dead_tup,\n       last_autovacuum, last_autoanalyze\n\u003cspan class=\"hljs-keyword\">FROM\u003c/span> pg_stat_user_tables\n\u003cspan class=\"hljs-keyword\">ORDER\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-keyword\">BY\u003c/span> n_dead_tup \u003cspan class=\"hljs-keyword\">DESC\u003c/span> LIMIT \u003cspan class=\"hljs-number\">10\u003c/span>;\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>\n\u003cp>A growing \u003ccode>n_dead_tup\u003c/code> with an old \u003ccode>last_autovacuum\u003c/code> means vacuum is behind. Track table and index size over time, because steady growth with a constant row count is bloat.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"what-blocks-cleanup\">What blocks cleanup\u003c/h2>\n\u003cp>Vacuum cannot remove a row version that some transaction might still see.\u003c/p>\n\u003cdiv class=\"code\" data-lang=\"sql\">\u003cbutton class=\"copy\" type=\"button\" data-copy=\"SELECT pid, now() - xact_start AS age, state, left(query, 60)\nFROM pg_stat_activity\nWHERE xact_start IS NOT NULL ORDER BY age DESC LIMIT 5;\n\nSELECT slot_name, active, restart_lsn FROM pg_replication_slots;\" aria-label=\"Copy code\">Copy\u003c/button>\u003cpre tabindex=\"0\">\u003ccode class=\"hljs\">\u003cspan class=\"hljs-keyword\">SELECT\u003c/span> pid, now() \u003cspan class=\"hljs-operator\">-\u003c/span> xact_start \u003cspan class=\"hljs-keyword\">AS\u003c/span> age, state, \u003cspan class=\"hljs-keyword\">left\u003c/span>(query, \u003cspan class=\"hljs-number\">60\u003c/span>)\n\u003cspan class=\"hljs-keyword\">FROM\u003c/span> pg_stat_activity\n\u003cspan class=\"hljs-keyword\">WHERE\u003c/span> xact_start \u003cspan class=\"hljs-keyword\">IS\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-keyword\">NOT NULL\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-keyword\">ORDER\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-keyword\">BY\u003c/span> age \u003cspan class=\"hljs-keyword\">DESC\u003c/span> LIMIT \u003cspan class=\"hljs-number\">5\u003c/span>;\n\n\u003cspan class=\"hljs-keyword\">SELECT\u003c/span> slot_name, active, restart_lsn \u003cspan class=\"hljs-keyword\">FROM\u003c/span> pg_replication_slots;\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>\n\u003cp>Long transactions, idle-in-transaction sessions and inactive replication slots are the usual causes. Fix them first, then tune.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"tuning\">Tuning\u003c/h2>\n\u003cdiv class=\"code\" data-lang=\"sql\">\u003cbutton class=\"copy\" type=\"button\" data-copy=\"ALTER TABLE events SET (\n  autovacuum_vacuum_scale_factor = 0.02,\n  autovacuum_analyze_scale_factor = 0.01,\n  autovacuum_vacuum_cost_limit = 2000\n);\" aria-label=\"Copy code\">Copy\u003c/button>\u003cpre tabindex=\"0\">\u003ccode class=\"hljs\">\u003cspan class=\"hljs-keyword\">ALTER TABLE\u003c/span> events \u003cspan class=\"hljs-keyword\">SET\u003c/span> (\n  autovacuum_vacuum_scale_factor \u003cspan class=\"hljs-operator\">=\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-number\">0.02\u003c/span>,\n  autovacuum_analyze_scale_factor \u003cspan class=\"hljs-operator\">=\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-number\">0.01\u003c/span>,\n  autovacuum_vacuum_cost_limit \u003cspan class=\"hljs-operator\">=\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-number\">2000\u003c/span>\n);\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>\n\u003cp>The default triggers at 20 percent dead rows, which is too late for huge tables. Lower the scale factor per hot table and raise the cost limit so vacuum works faster.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"wraparound-and-maintenance\">Wraparound and maintenance\u003c/h2>\n\u003cp>Transaction IDs are finite. Monitor \u003ccode>age(datfrozenxid)\u003c/code> and keep it far from the limit. To shrink an already bloated table, use \u003ccode>pg_repack\u003c/code> or plan a \u003ccode>VACUUM FULL\u003c/code>, which locks the table, in a maintenance window with a rollback plan.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"how-the-parts-fit-together\">How the parts fit together\u003c/h2>\n\u003cp>The lesson in one picture: each box is a section of this lesson, in the order you meet it. Select a box to see what it does and the mistake that usually goes with it.\u003c/p>\n\u003cfigure class=\"dg\" data-rows=\"2\" data-edges=\"n1&gt;n2;n2&gt;n3;n3&gt;n4\" style=\"--rows:2\">\u003cfigcaption class=\"dg-title\">How Autovacuum, bloat and maintenance windows fits together\u003c/figcaption>\u003cdiv class=\"dg-stage\">\u003csvg viewBox=\"0 0 100 100\" preserveAspectRatio=\"none\" aria-hidden=\"true\" focusable=\"false\">\u003cline x1=\"12.5\" y1=\"25.0\" x2=\"37.5\" y2=\"75.0\"/>\u003cline x1=\"37.5\" y1=\"75.0\" x2=\"62.5\" y2=\"25.0\"/>\u003cline x1=\"62.5\" y1=\"25.0\" x2=\"87.5\" y2=\"75.0\"/>\u003c/svg>\u003cbutton type=\"button\" class=\"dg-node k-process\" data-act=\"dg-pick\" data-id=\"n1\" data-kind=\"process\" style=\"left:12.5%;top:25.0%;--w:22.0%\" aria-pressed=\"false\">\u003cspan class=\"dg-name\">Watch vacuum\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"dg-kind\">Step\u003c/span>\u003c/button>\u003cbutton type=\"button\" class=\"dg-node k-process\" data-act=\"dg-pick\" data-id=\"n2\" data-kind=\"process\" style=\"left:37.5%;top:75.0%;--w:22.0%\" aria-pressed=\"false\">\u003cspan class=\"dg-name\">What blocks cleanup\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"dg-kind\">Step\u003c/span>\u003c/button>\u003cbutton type=\"button\" class=\"dg-node k-process\" data-act=\"dg-pick\" data-id=\"n3\" data-kind=\"process\" style=\"left:62.5%;top:25.0%;--w:22.0%\" aria-pressed=\"false\">\u003cspan class=\"dg-name\">Tuning\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"dg-kind\">Step\u003c/span>\u003c/button>\u003cbutton type=\"button\" class=\"dg-node k-process\" data-act=\"dg-pick\" data-id=\"n4\" data-kind=\"process\" style=\"left:87.5%;top:75.0%;--w:22.0%\" aria-pressed=\"false\">\u003cspan class=\"dg-name\">Wraparound and maintenance\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"dg-kind\">Step\u003c/span>\u003c/button>\u003c/div>\u003cp class=\"sr\">Flow in order: 1, Watch vacuum to What blocks cleanup; 2, What blocks cleanup to Tuning; 3, Tuning to Wraparound and maintenance.\u003c/p>\u003cul class=\"dg-legend\" aria-label=\"Legend\">\u003cli>\u003ci class=\"dg-sw k-process\" aria-hidden=\"true\">\u003c/i>Step\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003ci class=\"dg-arrow\" aria-hidden=\"true\">\u003c/i>Numbered arrows show the order of the flow\u003c/li>\u003c/ul>\u003cp class=\"dg-hint small\">Select a component to see what it does and what happens when it fails. \u003cbutton type=\"button\" class=\"btn btn-quiet dg-flow-btn\" data-act=\"dg-flow\" aria-pressed=\"false\">Show the flow\u003c/button>\u003c/p>\u003cdiv class=\"dg-live\" role=\"status\" aria-live=\"polite\">\u003c/div>\u003cdl class=\"dg-list\">\u003cdiv class=\"dg-item\" data-id=\"n1\">\u003cdt>Watch vacuum \u003cspan class=\"dg-kt\">Step\u003c/span>\u003c/dt>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">What it does:\u003c/span> A growing n_dead_tup with an old last_autovacuum means vacuum is behind.\u003c/dd>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">If it fails or is weak:\u003c/span> Only raising autovacuum frequency when something blocks it.\u003c/dd>\u003c/div>\u003cdiv class=\"dg-item\" data-id=\"n2\">\u003cdt>What blocks cleanup \u003cspan class=\"dg-kt\">Step\u003c/span>\u003c/dt>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">What it does:\u003c/span> Vacuum cannot remove a row version that some transaction might still see.\u003c/dd>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">If it fails or is weak:\u003c/span> Forgetting inactive replication slots.\u003c/dd>\u003c/div>\u003cdiv class=\"dg-item\" data-id=\"n3\">\u003cdt>Tuning \u003cspan class=\"dg-kt\">Step\u003c/span>\u003c/dt>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">What it does:\u003c/span> The default triggers at 20 percent dead rows, which is too late for huge tables.\u003c/dd>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">If it fails or is weak:\u003c/span> Running VACUUM FULL during business hours.\u003c/dd>\u003c/div>\u003cdiv class=\"dg-item\" data-id=\"n4\">\u003cdt>Wraparound and maintenance \u003cspan class=\"dg-kt\">Step\u003c/span>\u003c/dt>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">What it does:\u003c/span> Transaction IDs are finite. Monitor age(datfrozenxid) and keep it far from the limit.\u003c/dd>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">If it fails or is weak:\u003c/span> Ignoring transaction ID age.\u003c/dd>\u003c/div>\u003c/dl>\u003c/figure>\n\u003ch2 id=\"incident-the-transaction-that-never-ended\">Incident: The transaction that never ended\u003c/h2>\n\u003caside class=\"callout callout-incident\">\u003cdiv class=\"callout-title\">Incident\u003c/div>\u003cp>An idle transaction blocked cleanup for three weeks.\u003c/p>\n\u003c/aside>\n\u003cdiv class=\"code console\" data-lang=\"terminal\">\u003cpre tabindex=\"0\">\u003ccode class=\"hljs\">\u003cspan class=\"ln out\">pid 8841  idle in transaction  xact_start: 21 days ago\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln out\">n_dead_tup: 184220001  last_autovacuum: 2 minutes ago\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln out\">table size: 412 GB (live data about 40 GB)\u003c/span>\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>\n\u003cp>A script opened a transaction and never closed it. Autovacuum ran constantly but could not remove old rows. After the session was ended the team added idle_in_transaction_session_timeout and an alert on transactions older than 15 minutes.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"common-mistakes\">Common mistakes\u003c/h2>\n\u003cul>\n\u003cli>Only raising autovacuum frequency when something blocks it\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Forgetting inactive replication slots\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Running VACUUM FULL during business hours\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Ignoring transaction ID age\u003c/li>\n\u003c/ul>\n","toc":[{"id":"what-you-will-learn","text":"What you will learn"},{"id":"watch-vacuum","text":"Watch vacuum"},{"id":"what-blocks-cleanup","text":"What blocks cleanup"},{"id":"tuning","text":"Tuning"},{"id":"wraparound-and-maintenance","text":"Wraparound and maintenance"},{"id":"how-the-parts-fit-together","text":"How the parts fit together"},{"id":"incident-the-transaction-that-never-ended","text":"Incident: The transaction that never ended"},{"id":"common-mistakes","text":"Common mistakes"}],"refs":[{"t":"PostgreSQL routine vacuuming","u":"https://www.postgresql.org/docs/current/routine-vacuuming.html"},{"t":"PostgreSQL autovacuum configuration","u":"https://www.postgresql.org/docs/current/runtime-config-autovacuum.html"}],"text":"Updates and deletes in PostgreSQL create new row versions and leave old ones behind. Autovacuum reclaims them in the background. When it cannot keep up, or cannot remove rows because something still needs them, tables bloat and performance drops. What you will learn Dead tuples, bloat and what autovacuum does Finding what blocks vacuum Tuning autovacuum for busy tables Transaction ID wraparound and safe maintenance Watch vacuum SELECT relname, n_live_tup, n_dead_tup, last_autovacuum, last_autoanalyze FROM pg_stat_user_tables ORDER BY n_dead_tup DESC LIMIT 10 ; A growing n_dead_tup with an old last_autovacuum means vacuum is behind. Track table and index size over time, because steady growth with a constant row count is bloat. What blocks cleanup Vacuum cannot remove a row version that some transaction might still see. SELECT pid, now() - xact_start AS age, state, left (query, 60 ) FROM pg_stat_activity WHERE xact_start IS NOT NULL ORDER BY age DESC LIMIT 5 ; SELECT slot_name, active, restart_lsn FROM pg_replication_slots; Long transactions, idle-in-transaction sessions and inactive replication slots are the usual causes. Fix them first, then tune. Tuning ALTER TABLE events SET ( autovacuum_vacuum_scale_factor = 0.02 , autovacuum_analyze_scale_factor = 0.01 , autovacuum_vacuum_cost_limit = 2000 ); The default triggers at 20 percent dead rows, which is too late for huge tables. Lower the scale factor per hot table and raise the cost limit so vacuum works faster. Wraparound and maintenance Transaction IDs are finite. Monitor age(datfrozenxid) and keep it far from the limit. To shrink an already bloated table, use pg_repack or plan a VACUUM FULL , which locks the table, in a maintenance window with a rollback plan. How the parts fit together The lesson in one picture: each box is a section of this lesson, in the order you meet it. Select a box to see what it does and the mistake that usually goes with it. How Autovacuum, bloat and maintenance windows fits together Flow in order: 1, Watch vacuum to What blocks cleanup; 2, What blocks cleanup to Tuning; 3, Tuning to Wraparound and maintenance. Step Numbered arrows show the order of the flow Select a component to see what it does and what happens when it fails. Watch vacuum Step What it does: A growing n_dead_tup with an old last_autovacuum means vacuum is behind. If it fails or is weak: Only raising autovacuum frequency when something blocks it. What blocks cleanup Step What it does: Vacuum cannot remove a row version that some transaction might still see. If it fails or is weak: Forgetting inactive replication slots. Tuning Step What it does: The default triggers at 20 percent dead rows, which is too late for huge tables. If it fails or is weak: Running VACUUM FULL during business hours. Wraparound and maintenance Step What it does: Transaction IDs are finite. Monitor age(datfrozenxid) and keep it far from the limit. If it fails or is weak: Ignoring transaction ID age. Incident: The transaction that never ended Incident An idle transaction blocked cleanup for three weeks. pid 8841 idle in transaction xact_start: 21 days ago n_dead_tup: 184220001 last_autovacuum: 2 minutes ago table size: 412 GB (live data about 40 GB) A script opened a transaction and never closed it. Autovacuum ran constantly but could not remove old rows. After the session was ended the team added idle_in_transaction_session_timeout and an alert on transactions older than 15 minutes. Common mistakes Only raising autovacuum frequency when something blocks it Forgetting inactive replication slots Running VACUUM FULL during business hours Ignoring transaction ID age"},"sql-ops/database-incident-runbook":{"html":"\u003cp>During an incident people guess. A written order of checks removes guessing, shortens the time to a diagnosis and leaves evidence for the review afterwards.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"what-you-will-learn\">What you will learn\u003c/h2>\n\u003cul>\n\u003cli>The order of checks: scope, connections, locks, resources, replication, changes\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Queries that reveal each cause\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Choosing the smallest safe mitigation\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Capturing evidence for the postmortem\u003c/li>\n\u003c/ul>\n\u003ch2 id=\"runbook-order\">Runbook order\u003c/h2>\n\u003col>\n\u003cli>\u003cstrong>Scope:\u003c/strong> who is affected, since when, which endpoints\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>\u003cstrong>Connections:\u003c/strong> are we at the limit or waiting on a pool\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>\u003cstrong>Long queries and locks:\u003c/strong> who blocks whom\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>\u003cstrong>Resources:\u003c/strong> CPU, memory, disk space and I/O\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>\u003cstrong>Replication:\u003c/strong> lag, broken replica, stuck slot\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>\u003cstrong>Changes:\u003c/strong> deploys, migrations, config or traffic changes\u003c/li>\n\u003c/ol>\n\u003cdiv class=\"code\" data-lang=\"sql\">\u003cbutton class=\"copy\" type=\"button\" data-copy=\"-- connections by state\nSELECT state, count(*) FROM pg_stat_activity GROUP BY 1 ORDER BY 2 DESC;\n\n-- longest running queries\nSELECT pid, now() - query_start AS runtime, wait_event_type, left(query, 70)\nFROM pg_stat_activity WHERE state &lt;&gt; 'idle' ORDER BY runtime DESC LIMIT 5;\n\n-- who blocks whom\nSELECT pid, pg_blocking_pids(pid) AS blocked_by, left(query, 60)\nFROM pg_stat_activity WHERE cardinality(pg_blocking_pids(pid)) &gt; 0;\" aria-label=\"Copy code\">Copy\u003c/button>\u003cpre tabindex=\"0\">\u003ccode class=\"hljs\">\u003cspan class=\"hljs-comment\">-- connections by state\u003c/span>\n\u003cspan class=\"hljs-keyword\">SELECT\u003c/span> state, \u003cspan class=\"hljs-built_in\">count\u003c/span>(\u003cspan class=\"hljs-operator\">*\u003c/span>) \u003cspan class=\"hljs-keyword\">FROM\u003c/span> pg_stat_activity \u003cspan class=\"hljs-keyword\">GROUP\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-keyword\">BY\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-number\">1\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-keyword\">ORDER\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-keyword\">BY\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-number\">2\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-keyword\">DESC\u003c/span>;\n\n\u003cspan class=\"hljs-comment\">-- longest running queries\u003c/span>\n\u003cspan class=\"hljs-keyword\">SELECT\u003c/span> pid, now() \u003cspan class=\"hljs-operator\">-\u003c/span> query_start \u003cspan class=\"hljs-keyword\">AS\u003c/span> runtime, wait_event_type, \u003cspan class=\"hljs-keyword\">left\u003c/span>(query, \u003cspan class=\"hljs-number\">70\u003c/span>)\n\u003cspan class=\"hljs-keyword\">FROM\u003c/span> pg_stat_activity \u003cspan class=\"hljs-keyword\">WHERE\u003c/span> state \u003cspan class=\"hljs-operator\">&lt;&gt;\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">&#x27;idle&#x27;\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-keyword\">ORDER\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-keyword\">BY\u003c/span> runtime \u003cspan class=\"hljs-keyword\">DESC\u003c/span> LIMIT \u003cspan class=\"hljs-number\">5\u003c/span>;\n\n\u003cspan class=\"hljs-comment\">-- who blocks whom\u003c/span>\n\u003cspan class=\"hljs-keyword\">SELECT\u003c/span> pid, pg_blocking_pids(pid) \u003cspan class=\"hljs-keyword\">AS\u003c/span> blocked_by, \u003cspan class=\"hljs-keyword\">left\u003c/span>(query, \u003cspan class=\"hljs-number\">60\u003c/span>)\n\u003cspan class=\"hljs-keyword\">FROM\u003c/span> pg_stat_activity \u003cspan class=\"hljs-keyword\">WHERE\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-keyword\">cardinality\u003c/span>(pg_blocking_pids(pid)) \u003cspan class=\"hljs-operator\">&gt;\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-number\">0\u003c/span>;\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>\n\u003cdiv class=\"code\" data-lang=\"bash\">\u003cbutton class=\"copy\" type=\"button\" data-copy=\"df -h /var/lib/postgresql\niostat -x 1 3\" aria-label=\"Copy code\">Copy\u003c/button>\u003cpre tabindex=\"0\">\u003ccode class=\"hljs\">\u003cspan class=\"hljs-built_in\">df\u003c/span> -h /var/lib/postgresql\niostat -x 1 3\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>\n\u003ch2 id=\"mitigate\">Mitigate\u003c/h2>\n\u003cp>Pick the smallest safe action: cancel a runaway query with \u003ccode>pg_cancel_backend\u003c/code>, terminate a blocker with \u003ccode>pg_terminate_backend\u003c/code>, pause a job, shed load or fail over if the server is truly unhealthy. A full disk may need removing old logs or WAL carefully, never deleting files in the data directory by hand. Restarting loses caches and hides evidence, so it is rarely the first step.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"evidence\">Evidence\u003c/h2>\n\u003cp>Before changing anything, save the output of the queries above with a timestamp. Note each action and its effect. This record turns a stressful hour into a clear timeline for the review.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"how-the-parts-fit-together\">How the parts fit together\u003c/h2>\n\u003cp>The lesson in one picture: each box is a section of this lesson, in the order you meet it. Select a box to see what it does and the mistake that usually goes with it.\u003c/p>\n\u003cfigure class=\"dg\" data-rows=\"2\" data-edges=\"n1&gt;n2;n2&gt;n3\" style=\"--rows:2\">\u003cfigcaption class=\"dg-title\">How A database incident runbook fits together\u003c/figcaption>\u003cdiv class=\"dg-stage\">\u003csvg viewBox=\"0 0 100 100\" preserveAspectRatio=\"none\" aria-hidden=\"true\" focusable=\"false\">\u003cline x1=\"16.7\" y1=\"25.0\" x2=\"50.0\" y2=\"75.0\"/>\u003cline x1=\"50.0\" y1=\"75.0\" x2=\"83.3\" y2=\"25.0\"/>\u003c/svg>\u003cbutton type=\"button\" class=\"dg-node k-process\" data-act=\"dg-pick\" data-id=\"n1\" data-kind=\"process\" style=\"left:16.7%;top:25.0%;--w:29.3%\" aria-pressed=\"false\">\u003cspan class=\"dg-name\">Runbook order\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"dg-kind\">Step\u003c/span>\u003c/button>\u003cbutton type=\"button\" class=\"dg-node k-process\" data-act=\"dg-pick\" data-id=\"n2\" data-kind=\"process\" style=\"left:50.0%;top:75.0%;--w:29.3%\" aria-pressed=\"false\">\u003cspan class=\"dg-name\">Mitigate\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"dg-kind\">Step\u003c/span>\u003c/button>\u003cbutton type=\"button\" class=\"dg-node k-process\" data-act=\"dg-pick\" data-id=\"n3\" data-kind=\"process\" style=\"left:83.3%;top:25.0%;--w:29.3%\" aria-pressed=\"false\">\u003cspan class=\"dg-name\">Evidence\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"dg-kind\">Step\u003c/span>\u003c/button>\u003c/div>\u003cp class=\"sr\">Flow in order: 1, Runbook order to Mitigate; 2, Mitigate to Evidence.\u003c/p>\u003cul class=\"dg-legend\" aria-label=\"Legend\">\u003cli>\u003ci class=\"dg-sw k-process\" aria-hidden=\"true\">\u003c/i>Step\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003ci class=\"dg-arrow\" aria-hidden=\"true\">\u003c/i>Numbered arrows show the order of the flow\u003c/li>\u003c/ul>\u003cp class=\"dg-hint small\">Select a component to see what it does and what happens when it fails. \u003cbutton type=\"button\" class=\"btn btn-quiet dg-flow-btn\" data-act=\"dg-flow\" aria-pressed=\"false\">Show the flow\u003c/button>\u003c/p>\u003cdiv class=\"dg-live\" role=\"status\" aria-live=\"polite\">\u003c/div>\u003cdl class=\"dg-list\">\u003cdiv class=\"dg-item\" data-id=\"n1\">\u003cdt>Runbook order \u003cspan class=\"dg-kt\">Step\u003c/span>\u003c/dt>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">What it does:\u003c/span> Scope: who is affected, since when, which endpoints.\u003c/dd>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">If it fails or is weak:\u003c/span> Deleting WAL or data files to free space.\u003c/dd>\u003c/div>\u003cdiv class=\"dg-item\" data-id=\"n2\">\u003cdt>Mitigate \u003cspan class=\"dg-kt\">Step\u003c/span>\u003c/dt>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">What it does:\u003c/span> Pick the smallest safe action: cancel a runaway query with pg_cancel_backend, terminate a blocker with pg_terminate_backend, pause a job, shed load or fail over if the...\u003c/dd>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">If it fails or is weak:\u003c/span> Restarting before collecting evidence.\u003c/dd>\u003c/div>\u003cdiv class=\"dg-item\" data-id=\"n3\">\u003cdt>Evidence \u003cspan class=\"dg-kt\">Step\u003c/span>\u003c/dt>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">What it does:\u003c/span> Before changing anything, save the output of the queries above with a timestamp.\u003c/dd>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">If it fails or is weak:\u003c/span> Killing queries without finding the blocker.\u003c/dd>\u003c/div>\u003c/dl>\u003c/figure>\n\u003ch2 id=\"incident-afternoon-latency-spikes\">Incident: Afternoon latency spikes\u003c/h2>\n\u003caside class=\"callout callout-incident\">\u003cdiv class=\"callout-title\">Incident\u003c/div>\u003cp>A report query held locks and used all connections each afternoon.\u003c/p>\n\u003c/aside>\n\u003cdiv class=\"code console\" data-lang=\"terminal\">\u003cpre tabindex=\"0\">\u003ccode class=\"hljs\">\u003cspan class=\"ln out\">active: 96 of 100 connections\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln out\">pid 7314  runtime 00:41:12  report_daily_totals\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln out\">54 queries blocked by pid 7314\u003c/span>\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>\n\u003cp>An analyst&#39;s report ran in a transaction with an update, blocking the application. The on-call engineer cancelled it, saved the output, and the team moved reporting to a replica with a statement timeout and a lower connection limit for the reporting role.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"common-mistakes\">Common mistakes\u003c/h2>\n\u003cul>\n\u003cli>Restarting before collecting evidence\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Deleting WAL or data files to free space\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Killing queries without finding the blocker\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Skipping the review afterwards\u003c/li>\n\u003c/ul>\n","toc":[{"id":"what-you-will-learn","text":"What you will learn"},{"id":"runbook-order","text":"Runbook order"},{"id":"mitigate","text":"Mitigate"},{"id":"evidence","text":"Evidence"},{"id":"how-the-parts-fit-together","text":"How the parts fit together"},{"id":"incident-afternoon-latency-spikes","text":"Incident: Afternoon latency spikes"},{"id":"common-mistakes","text":"Common mistakes"}],"refs":[{"t":"PostgreSQL monitoring statistics","u":"https://www.postgresql.org/docs/current/monitoring-stats.html"},{"t":"PostgreSQL pg_locks view","u":"https://www.postgresql.org/docs/current/view-pg-locks.html"}],"text":"During an incident people guess. A written order of checks removes guessing, shortens the time to a diagnosis and leaves evidence for the review afterwards. What you will learn The order of checks: scope, connections, locks, resources, replication, changes Queries that reveal each cause Choosing the smallest safe mitigation Capturing evidence for the postmortem Runbook order Scope: who is affected, since when, which endpoints Connections: are we at the limit or waiting on a pool Long queries and locks: who blocks whom Resources: CPU, memory, disk space and I/O Replication: lag, broken replica, stuck slot Changes: deploys, migrations, config or traffic changes -- connections by state SELECT state, count ( * ) FROM pg_stat_activity GROUP BY 1 ORDER BY 2 DESC ; -- longest running queries SELECT pid, now() - query_start AS runtime, wait_event_type, left (query, 70 ) FROM pg_stat_activity WHERE state \u003c> &#x27;idle&#x27; ORDER BY runtime DESC LIMIT 5 ; -- who blocks whom SELECT pid, pg_blocking_pids(pid) AS blocked_by, left (query, 60 ) FROM pg_stat_activity WHERE cardinality (pg_blocking_pids(pid)) > 0 ; df -h /var/lib/postgresql iostat -x 1 3 Mitigate Pick the smallest safe action: cancel a runaway query with pg_cancel_backend , terminate a blocker with pg_terminate_backend , pause a job, shed load or fail over if the server is truly unhealthy. A full disk may need removing old logs or WAL carefully, never deleting files in the data directory by hand. Restarting loses caches and hides evidence, so it is rarely the first step. Evidence Before changing anything, save the output of the queries above with a timestamp. Note each action and its effect. This record turns a stressful hour into a clear timeline for the review. How the parts fit together The lesson in one picture: each box is a section of this lesson, in the order you meet it. Select a box to see what it does and the mistake that usually goes with it. How A database incident runbook fits together Flow in order: 1, Runbook order to Mitigate; 2, Mitigate to Evidence. Step Numbered arrows show the order of the flow Select a component to see what it does and what happens when it fails. Runbook order Step What it does: Scope: who is affected, since when, which endpoints. If it fails or is weak: Deleting WAL or data files to free space. Mitigate Step What it does: Pick the smallest safe action: cancel a runaway query with pg_cancel_backend, terminate a blocker with pg_terminate_backend, pause a job, shed load or fail over if the... If it fails or is weak: Restarting before collecting evidence. Evidence Step What it does: Before changing anything, save the output of the queries above with a timestamp. If it fails or is weak: Killing queries without finding the blocker. Incident: Afternoon latency spikes Incident A report query held locks and used all connections each afternoon. active: 96 of 100 connections pid 7314 runtime 00:41:12 report_daily_totals 54 queries blocked by pid 7314 An analyst&#39;s report ran in a transaction with an update, blocking the application. The on-call engineer cancelled it, saved the output, and the team moved reporting to a replica with a statement timeout and a lower connection limit for the reporting role. Common mistakes Restarting before collecting evidence Deleting WAL or data files to free space Killing queries without finding the blocker Skipping the review afterwards"}});